{"kind":"producing-content.farm.research-packet","generatedAt":"2026-07-19T15:04:03.000Z","term":"geometry football cup final","beat":"decolonization","queryTerms":["geometry","football","cup","final"],"sourceCount":106,"selectedCount":6,"intakeSources":[{"kind":"producing-content.farm.research-packet.source","subject":"geometry-football-and-the-world-cup-final","wire":"decolonization","path":"urn:newscable-bundle:ff5fe6e0a8620de31d0173b9d9e8334955ad1f4f83799aaae9b8192603972f1c","title":"Geometry, football and the World Cup final","url":"https://theconversation.com/geometry-football-and-the-world-cup-final-287792","domain":"theconversation.com","published":"2026-07-17T14:08:05Z","assertedAt":"2026-07-19T15:04:15.228Z","score":90,"matchedTerms":["geometry","football","cup","final"],"summary":"<p>Spain will play Argentina in the Fifa World Cup final on Sunday. The highly anticipated match takes place in New Jerseyâ€™s MetLife Stadium, with the teams vying for the most coveted prize in football.</p> <p>Attacking flair and physicality will no doubt play a role. But we could also see an underappreciated element of football come to the fore: <a href=\"https://theconversation.com/topics/geometry-8213\">geometry</a>.</p> <p>In their <a href=\"https://www.reuters.com/sports/soccer/spain-choke-life-out-france-reach-world-cup-final-2026-07-14/\">2-0 World Cup semi-final win over France</a>, Spain formed neat passing triangles around the French players. This sometimes made it look as if Spain had extra people on the field. </p> <p>Wherever a Spanish player received the ball, two teammates appeared at different angles â€“ with their French opponents seemingly always arriving a fraction too late.</p> <p>The triangle mattered not because it was neat, but because it gave Spain choice and France uncertainty. Each pass forced a French defender to commit to one possible future: press the ball, block the inside route, or protect the space behind. By the time that choice was made, the ball had often moved and the geometry had changed again.</p> <p>Spain were not simply finding space. They were controlling when and where the next space would appear.</p> <h2>Forcing choices</h2> <p>Put three attackers in a straight line and one defender can often block both supporting players. But move one supporting player sideways and the passing lines separate.</p> <p>The defender must choose which route to close. Press the ball and a pass can go around them. Protect the inside route and the outside may open. If another defender steps across, a Spanish player can attack the space left behind.</p> <p>A useful football triangle does not need equal sides or 60-degree angles. Its best shape changes with defendersâ€™ positions, body orientation, ball speed and the space available.</p> <p>A narrow triangle is easy to squeeze because one defender may block both lanes. A wide one makes the pass travel farther, giving an opponent more time to intercept it.</p> <p>In their semi-final match against France, Spain found the middle ground again and again â€“ close enough for quick combinations, but far enough apart to stretch Franceâ€™s press.</p> <h2>A pitch made of probabilities</h2> <p>Football geometry is a problem of time as well as space. A passing lane is not open simply because no defender is standing in it. The key question is whether the ball reaches the receiver before an opponent reaches the interception point. </p> <p>A diagram shows where players are â€“ but a useful model of the game asks where players can get to. <a href=\"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-021-97537-4\">Research using player-tracking data</a> estimates control of the pitch from playersâ€™ position, direction, speed and acceleration. The result is a constantly changing map of who is most likely to reach each area first. </p> <p>Pedro Porroâ€™s <a href=\"https://apnews.com/article/france-spain-world-cup-score-87fb7740fa552edf4bfd28d0e8727c23\">58th-minute semi-final goal</a> offered a compact example when, after exchanging passes with teammate Dani Olmo, he continued his run to score Spainâ€™s second. The first pass shifted the defendersâ€™ focus. Porroâ€™s movement created a new angle for the return.</p> <h2>A living network</h2> <p>Three-player combinations make this idea even harder to defend. Spainâ€™s strength was not just one triangle, but the speed with which several overlapped. A midfielder could be one corner of multiple shapes, connected to a defender, full-back, winger and another midfielder.</p> <p><a href=\"https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371%2Fjournal.pone.0171156\">Passing-network research</a> describes players as points and passes as links. Yet a final network diagram for a given football match misses something important: Spain were rebuilding those links with every movement and first touch they made. </p> <p>The value of a pass is not only where the ball goes, but how it changes what could happen next. That is why ten sideways passes are not automatically better than one pass that removes two defenders.</p> <h2>The shape that conquered basketball</h2> <p>Triangles are not unique to football. Under head coach Phil Jackson (1987-1998), US basketball team the Chicago Bulls made frequent use of a tactic called <a href=\"https://www.hoophall.com/hall-of-famers/tex-winter\">Tex Winterâ€™s triangle offence</a>. This places three players on one side of the court to create passing, cutting and scoring routes. </p> <p>The sport was different but the principle was the same: triangles preserve alternatives. Close one route and another remains; follow the ball and space opens elsewhere.</p> <p>In their match against Spain, Franceâ€™s problem was not simply a lack of running. Pressing works only when several defenders move together and close connected passing options. </p> <p>After the match, Kylian MbappÃ© said there had been <a href=\"https://www.reuters.com/sports/soccer/spain-choke-life-out-france-reach-world-cup-final-2026-07-14/\">â€œa lack of communicationâ€ in Franceâ€™s pressing</a>. Spain kept moving before France could reorganise.</p> <p>A triangle on a tactics board does nothing by itself. Its power depends on players scanning before receiving, opening their bodies, and using the first touch to preserve the option of more than one next action. </p> <p>The deeper contest was between Spainâ€™s speed of reconfiguration and Franceâ€™s speed of recognition. Spain kept changing the problem faster than France could solve it. The triangles were visible â€“ but their real advantage was control over what could happen next.</p> <p>Will we see a similar approach deployed in the final? Argentina will no doubt be closely studying their opponentâ€™s tactics from other matches. But geometry will still play a prominent role â€“ and may even prove a deciding factor.</p><img src=\"https://counter.theconversation.com/content/287792/count.gif\" alt=\"The Conversation\" width=\"1\" height=\"1\" /> <p class=\"fine-print\"><em><span>Conor Boland does not work for, consult, own shares in or receive funding from any company or organisation that would benefit from this article, and has disclosed no relevant affiliations beyond their academic appointment.</span></em></p>"},{"kind":"producing-content.farm.research-packet.source","subject":"what-cabo-verde-s-world-cup-debut-means-for-african-football","wire":"decolonization","path":"urn:newscable-bundle:cf1386928efca25323a212d369c6edb1af227d519d2319aebf4860bed4d2bbda","title":"What Cabo Verdeâ€™s World Cup debut means for African football","url":"https://africasacountry.com/2026/07/what-cabo-verdes-world-cup-debut-means-for-african-football","domain":"africasacountry.com","published":"2026-07-10T06:15:41.504236Z","assertedAt":"2026-07-19T15:04:15.228Z","score":30,"matchedTerms":["football","cup","final"],"summary":"It happened. Cabo Verde lost to Argentina and exited the 2026 FIFA World Cup. And I couldnâ€™t be prouder. As a former football player, lifelong lover of the beautiful game, and Cabo Verdean-American, watching Cabo Verde compete on the world stage for the first time felt bigger than sports. What this team represented went far beyond the final score. At the start of the tournament, predictive models gave Cabo Verde almost no chance against former world champions Spain. The result: a stunning 0â€“0 draw anchored by seven saves from goalkeeper Josimar â€œVozinhaâ€ Dias. Against Uruguay, analysts dismissed Cabo Verdeâ€™s disciplined defensive strategy as no match for Marcelo Bielsaâ€™s aggressive attacking system. Commentators insisted Cabo Verde would never score. The result: a thrilling 2â€“2 draw, including the nationâ€™s first-ever World Cup goal from midfielder Kevin Pina. Then came another scoreless draw against Saudi Arabia, securing Cabo Verde a second-place group finish and a historic place as the smallest nation ever to reach the knockout rounds. By the time Cabo Verde faced defending champions Argentina, the world was paying attention and rooting for the underdogs. And for a moment, many of us believed the impossible could happen. When Deroy Duarte equalized to make it 1â€“1, we felt hope. When Sidny Lopes Cabral scored again to make it 2â€“2, belief swept across the world. Even after Argentina secured a 3â€“2 victory in extra time, Cabo Verde exited the tournament undefeated at the 90-minute mark against three former world champions. The loss hurt deeply. But not our pride. We may not have won on the scoreboard, but we won the respect of football fans around the world. In the days following the match, widely shared reports estimated that the Argentina versus Cabo Verde match attracted as many as 2.7 billion viewers worldwide. While those figures have not yet been officially verified by FIFA, the extraordinary claim reflected something that was unmistakably true: millions of people had fallen in love with Cabo Verdeâ€™s Cinderella story. For a nation of just over 500,000 people, that kind of global attention is extraordinary. Cabo Verde, an archipelago of 10 islands, was previously colonized by Portugal in the fifteenth century. It was a central port in the transatlantic slave trade before gaining independence in 1975. That independence came through the liberation struggle led by AmÃ­lcar Cabral, whose African Party for the Independence of Guinea and Cape Verde (PAIGC) fought Portuguese colonialism across both territories. Although Cabral was assassinated in 1973, two years before the independence, he helped make Cabo Verdeâ€™s independence possible, and remains one of Africaâ€™s most influential revolutionary thinkers. Today, the Cabo Verdean diaspora is estimated at roughly one million people, nearly double the resident population. More Cabo Verdeans now live in the United States, Portugal, the Netherlands, France, Brazil, Senegal, Angola, and elsewhere other than on the islands themselves. For decades, Cabo Verde existed on the margins of global football. The national team first entered FIFA World Cup qualification during the 2002 cycle and spent years competing against larger, wealthier, and better-funded football nations before finally qualifying for the 2026 tournament. The turning point came in the 2010s. The Blue Sharks qualified for their first Africa Cup of Nations in 2013 and stunned the continent by reaching the quarterfinals. Since then Cabo Verde has become regular AFCON competitors, and made their return to the quarterfinals in 2023. This was not a team built on global superstars or massive sponsorships. Many players came from lower-division clubs across Europe and the diaspora. Some were recruited through personal networks and online outreach, including LinkedIn, where the federation famously connected with Irish-based defender Roberto â€œPicoâ€ Lopes after he initially overlooked an earlier message written in Portuguese. What united the squad was discipline, camaraderie, and belief. Under manager Pedro â€œBubistaâ€ LeitÃ£o Brito, Cabo Verde played with tactical structure, composure, courage, and intelligence. In doing so, they challenged many of the assumptions often placed on African teams and Black athletes across sports: that they lack tactical discipline; that they rely more on physicality than strategy; or that they cannot compete at the highest level. Instead, Cabo Verde demonstrated that talent exists everywhere, even when visibility, infrastructure, and resources do not. One of the clearest examples of that visibility came through Vozinha. Before the World Cup, the 40-year-old goalkeeper was approaching retirement with a modest social media following of roughly 50,000 people. By the end of Cabo Verdeâ€™s historic run, he had become one of the breakout stars of the tournament and the most-followed goalkeeper in the world, with more than 26 million Instagram followers. Visibility changes careers. It creates sponsorship opportunities, attracts media attention, opens commercial doors, and reshapes how athletes, and the nations they represent, are perceived around the world. But this story is bigger than one player or one nation. The expanded World Cup also brought unprecedented visibility to African and Caribbean teams including Morocco, Senegal, Ghana, Egypt, Algeria, Tunisia, South Africa, DR Congo, CÃ´te dâ€™Ivoire, Haiti, and CuraÃ§ao. These nations were expected merely to make an appearance. Instead, they arrived prepared to compete. And that visibility matters. For a few extraordinary weeks, the world saw Cabo Verde not as a footnote, but as a nation worthy of respect, admiration, and serious attention. Together with the other African and Caribbean nations, they helped reshape the global conversation about the expanded 48-team tournament, the continued socio-political and economic realities facing emerging football nations, and the transformative power of access, opportunity, and visibility. In the words of Cabo Verde manager Bubista: â€œWe wanted to show that you can achieve great things regardless of your challenges, just as long as you have a dream and chase after it.â€ Thank you, Cabo Verde. And thank you to every African and Caribbean nation that stepped onto the world stage. Your fearlessness, resilience, and courage reminded the world that greatness has never been limited by geography. The future of African football is bright."},{"kind":"producing-content.farm.research-packet.source","subject":"tactically-complete-spain-vs-an-inspired-messi-led-argentina-who-will-win-the-world-cup-final","wire":"decolonization","path":"urn:newscable-bundle:bd56a11c18dd8d72e7ec69c15d4be07cccc57904367b1eecc277abfe2ebca4a4","title":"â€˜Tactically completeâ€™ Spain vs an inspired Messi-led Argentina. Who will win the World Cup final?","url":"https://theconversation.com/tactically-complete-spain-vs-an-inspired-messi-led-argentina-who-will-win-the-world-cup-final-287372","domain":"theconversation.com","published":"2026-07-17T02:59:08Z","assertedAt":"2026-07-19T15:04:15.228Z","score":30,"matchedTerms":["football","cup","final"],"summary":"<p>A record 102 matches have been played, leaving just two games to decide the <a href=\"https://theconversation.com/topics/fifa-world-cup-2026-186405\">2026 FIFA World Cup</a>: the third-place playoff (between France and England) and the final. </p> <p>The championship match, <a href=\"https://www.sbs.com.au/ondemand/collection/fifa-world-cup-2026-live-and-upcoming\">to be played on Sunday evening US time</a>, will see Spain take on Argentina in a fitting finale between <a href=\"https://inside.fifa.com/fifa-world-ranking/men\">the worldâ€™s two highest-ranked teams</a>. </p> <p>So, how did each team reach the final â€“ and how may it play out?</p> <p></p> <h2>How we got here</h2> <p>Both sides enter the final unbeaten after seven matches. </p> <p>Spain arrive as the reigning European champions and the worldâ€™s number two-ranked nation, while Argentina is the reigning Copa AmÃ©rica champion, defending World Cup holders and ranked first in the world.</p> <p>Spain are arguably <a href=\"https://www.nytimes.com/athletic/7446280/2026/07/15/spain-france-semifinal-tactical-analysis/\">the tournamentâ€™s most tactically complete side</a>. </p> <p>Luis de la Fuenteâ€™s team has been built on patient possession, relentless pressing and exceptional defensive organisation. </p> <p>They <a href=\"https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/articles/cpv3v0gkw9vo\">dismantled France 2-0 in the semi-final</a>, conceding very few clear chances, and have allowed just one goal throughout the tournament. In sports science terms, Spain have built towards peak performance for the final, after opening the tournament with a <a href=\"https://edition.cnn.com/2026/06/21/sport/spain-world-cup-ambitions-cape-verde\">draw against Cape Verde</a>. </p> <p>What has characterised the Spaniards is that no single player has stood above the rest â€“ the teamâ€™s success has been shared across the squad. </p> <p>This is a complete team coached by a manager at the peak of his powers. Six players have scored at least one goal, although Mikel Oyarzabal has scored five.</p> <p>Goalkeeper Unai SimÃ³n has been a rock and made some brilliant saves, and used intelligent positioning outside the box. Dani Olmo, despite being substituted twice, has been a constant attacking threat, while captain Rodri has also gone about his work with little fanfare, marshalling the team superbly from midfield. </p> <p></p> <h2>The Messi factor</h2> <p>Argentina is also a great team, but a different type of great team, and there is one standout player.</p> <p>There is little left to write about Lionel Messi that has not already been said. Yet the brilliance of this Argentine team is not simply built around his genius â€“ it is built around the extraordinary commitment of the ten players around him. </p> <hr> <p> <em> <strong> Read more: <a href=\"https://theconversation.com/messi-is-old-short-and-slow-how-is-he-still-dominating-at-the-world-cup-286238\">Messi is old, short and slow. How is he still dominating at the World Cup?</a> </strong> </em> </p> <hr> <p>They run for him, defend for him, sacrifice for him, and believe in him completely. </p> <p>Messi is afforded the freedom to conserve his energy, drifting into the spaces where he can do the greatest damage. His teammates carry the physical burden, allowing their captain to deliver moments of magic when they matter most. </p> <p>He is not merely Argentinaâ€™s leader â€“ he is their inspiration, their symbol, and the figure who carries the hopes of an entire nation. Throughout the tournament, whenever Argentina faced a moment of crisis, the players turned to Messi with the belief he would find a way.</p> <p>He nearly always does, and <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/football/2026/jul/16/england-appeared-to-have-lionel-messi-under-control-until-the-crucial-moments\">he did it again</a> in their unforgettable victory over England. </p> <p>That win represented more than qualification for another final. For many Argentinians, it carried the emotional weight of <a href=\"https://www.goal.com/en-au/lists/lionel-messi-argentina-england-diego-maradona-tribute-world-cup-semi-final/blt8cb51a8577016719\">Diego Maradonaâ€™s legacy</a> and offered the opportunity to defeat England on soccerâ€™s biggest stage through skill and determination rather than controversy. </p> <p>It was another defining chapter in Argentinaâ€™s remarkable World Cup story.</p> <p></p> <h2>How will the final play out?</h2> <p>Tactically, the match promises a fascinating contrast.</p> <p>Spain will seek to monopolise possession, patiently moving the ball until openings appear, while Argentina is comfortable defending compactly before striking quickly (usually through Messi).</p> <p>Whichever side controls the tempo in midfield is likely to have one hand on the trophy.</p> <p>Spain will try and play with the finesse they showed against the French, and Rodri will try to ensure they avoid a â€œstreet fightâ€, which the Argentinians may try and bait them into.</p> <p>History also adds another layer. Spain is chasing its second World Cup after the triumph in 2010, while Argentina is pursuing a fourth title and back-to-back World Cups â€“ an achievement not secured in more than six decades. </p> <p>The final therefore offers Spain the chance to cement a new era of dominance, while Argentina seek to extend one of the greatest periods in its history.</p> <p>It is a fitting climax to the tournament: the worldâ€™s number one-ranked team against the ranked team second. The defending champions against the European champions, and almost certainly Messiâ€™s final World Cup appearance.</p> <p></p> <p>In a sport now dominated by players who run all day and are increasingly similar, with an abundance of machine-like footballers and fewer players blessed with extraordinary skill and tactical understanding, this World Cup has produced some remarkable goals and performances.</p> <p>Harry Kane, Jude Bellingham, Erling Haaland and Kylian MbappÃ© have all produced moments of brilliance.</p> <p>But the final 25 minutes of the semi-final between England and Argentina, after England had taken a 1-0 lead, demonstrated that there has never been, and perhaps never will be, another player quite like Messi.</p> <p>We may be about to marvel at Messi for the last time. It is hard to believe he will not find a way to overcome this outstanding Spanish team.</p><img src=\"https://counter.theconversation.com/content/287372/count.gif\" alt=\"The Conversation\" width=\"1\" height=\"1\" /> <p class=\"fine-print\"><em><span>Steve Georgakis does not work for, consult, own shares in or receive funding from any company or organisation that would benefit from this article, and has disclosed no relevant affiliations beyond their academic appointment.</span></em></p>"},{"kind":"producing-content.farm.research-packet.source","subject":"world-cup-var-controversies-show-why-human-referees-should-decide-where-potential-fouls-begin","wire":"decolonization","path":"urn:newscable-bundle:3912c39c8a774db9c8aefeef3066eac091398e637854dfbbbc2c2ebbc3ecf1b3","title":"World Cup VAR controversies show why human referees should decide where potential fouls begin","url":"https://theconversation.com/world-cup-var-controversies-show-why-human-referees-should-decide-where-potential-fouls-begin-287676","domain":"theconversation.com","published":"2026-07-16T21:22:02Z","assertedAt":"2026-07-19T15:04:15.228Z","score":30,"matchedTerms":["football","cup","final"],"summary":"<p>Two contested decisions at the 2026 World Cup <a href=\"https://www.reuters.com/sports/soccer/vargentina-reach-semi-finals-under-cloud-complaints-2026-07-13/\">have reignited debate</a> over how video assistant referee (VAR) reviews should work, and how far back a referee should look when deciding what counts as part of the same passage of play.</p> <p>VAR allows referees to review contentious incidents using replay footage, <a href=\"https://www.theifab.com/laws/latest/video-assistant-referee-var-protocol/\">with the human referee making the final decision</a>. Itâ€™s meant to catch â€œclear and obvious errorsâ€ or incidents that the on-field officials missed. A referee reviewing an incident must decide how far back in time the reviewable passage of play extends.</p> <p>A 2021 <a href=\"https://fis.dshs-koeln.de/en/publications/video-assistant-referees-var-the-impact-of-technology-on-decision/\">study of 2,195 competitive football matches across 13 countries</a> found referees got calls right 92.1 per cent of the time, rising to 98.3 per cent after a VAR intervention. </p> <p>But replay only supplies the evidence. It doesnâ€™t supply interpretation, responsibility or a decision about how much of the play needs to be examined to establish appropriate context. That judgment falls to the referee. VARâ€™s effectiveness depends on referees interpreting the evidence within the incidentâ€™s interactions and its relevant context.</p> <p>As researchers <a href=\"https://digital.la84.org/digital/collection/p17103coll10/id/24101/\">who study the ethical implications of AI use in soccer</a>, weâ€™ve found that two decisions from the 2026 tournament illustrate how difficult these boundary calls can be in practice.</p> <h2>Argentinaâ€™s overturned goal</h2> <p>In the round of 16 match between Argentina and Egypt, VAR overturned an Egyptian goal after reviewing an incident that occurred nearly a minute earlier.</p> <p>Egypt was leading 1-0 when Mostafa Ziko scored in the 58th minute to make it 2-0. VAR recommended an on-field review of Egyptâ€™s Marwan Attia stepping on Argentinaâ€™s Lisandro MartÃ­nezâ€™s foot <a href=\"https://observer.co.uk/news/national/article/red-cards-var-disallowed-goals-conspiracy-theories-thrive-at-the-world-cup\">17 seconds before the goal</a> and more than 80 metres from where it was scored. </p> <p>The referee reviewed the incident and cancelled the goal. Argentina came back to win the game 3-2. </p> <p>The integral question is whether that foot-stepping incident still counted as being connected to the goal a minute later, or whether play had moved on enough to be treated as over and done with.</p> <p><a href=\"https://inside.fifa.com/refereeing/news/pierluigi-collina-we-are-not-influenced\">FIFA, soccerâ€™s governing body, says</a> an attacking phase in a game has no pre-set time or distance limit. </p> <p>Providing commentary on the situation, former referee <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/football/2026/jul/08/refereeing-world-cup-fine-input-tuchel-trump-comment\">Chris Foy argued the play should count as one continuous move</a> because the ball kept travelling forward. Another former referee, <a href=\"https://www.foxsports.com/stories/soccer/egypts-goal-vs-argentina-disallowed-after-var-check-not-why-var-brought-game\">Mark Clattenburg, disagreed</a>, arguing the distance covered and the time that had passed were enough to break the connection between the foul and the goal that followed.</p> <p>Egypt also appealed for a penalty late in the match, <a href=\"https://www.espn.com/soccer/story/_/id/49312344/pierluigi-collina-defends-egypt-argentina-referee-calls-send-home-world-cup\">after Mohamed Salah went down following contact with JuliÃ¡n Ãlvarez</a>, but it didnâ€™t prompt an on-field review; <a href=\"https://inside.fifa.com/refereeing/news/pierluigi-collina-we-are-not-influenced\">FIFA said</a> the defender played the ball first before normal contact followed. </p> <p>Both decisions may be legally defensible, but their different treatment shows why officials should explain where their review began, why the earlier act remained relevant and why one incident reached the VAR monitor while the other did not.</p> <h2>Haaland push overturns Norwayâ€™s goal</h2> <p>A second case, from Norwayâ€™s World Cup quarterfinal against England, raises a similar question: does a VAR review apply before the ball is back in play? </p> <p>TorbjÃ¸rn Heggem appeared to put Norway ahead in the 55th minute of the game, scoring on a rebound from a corner kick. </p> <p>But replays showed that as the corner was being delivered, Norwayâ€™s <a href=\"https://www.the-independent.com/sport/football/norway-england-goal-erling-haaland-foul-var-world-cup-b3013299.html\">Erling Haaland had extended both his arms and pushed Englandâ€™s Elliot Anderson to the ground</a>. The two had been <a href=\"https://www.si.com/soccer/how-new-world-cup-rule-england-vs-norway-world-cup-quarterfinal\">jostling for position</a> prior to the moment of contact, and Haaland appeared to be reacting to Anderson.</p> <p>The ball was not yet in play when the push occurred, but officials judged it had a direct causal link to the goal that followed: Haaland remained involved as the ball arrived from the corner kick, which fell to Patrick Berg. His shot was saved by goalkeeper Jordan Pickford, and the rebound fell to Heggem, who scored.</p> <p>Under a World Cup clarification, an attacking offence before a restart of play <a href=\"https://www.theifab.com/news/the-ifab-clarifies-var-protocol-for-attacking-team-offences-before-the-ball-is-in/\">may be reviewed if it directly affects a goal</a>. After review, the goal was cancelled and the corner retaken. Norway lost the match 2-1 after extra time.</p> <p>Questions were nonetheless asked about when the review began, since it did not appear to include Andersonâ€™s own physical duel with Haaland in the moments before the push. That earlier jostling does not necessarily mean Anderson provoked Haaland, but it is a reminder that the referee wasnâ€™t reviewing an isolated fact off a VAR screen. </p> <p>Neither case necessarily proves the refereesâ€™ decisions wrong, but both show that defining the relevant incident remains part of human refereeing.</p> <h2>Where does an incident begin?</h2> <p>VARâ€™s core problem is, in fact, defining the event under review: how far it extends through time and across the pitch, and where the causal connection ends. This requires reading whether ball possession, player positions and opportunities to challenge still carry the effects of an earlier incident, or whether the game has moved on and reset. </p> <p>Too narrow a frame isolates an action from the broader context that makes it significant. Too broad a frame drags in remote events after a new phase of the game has begun. </p> <p>Footage is not self-explanatory. Referees and video officials choose duration, angle, speed and relevant interactions, turning play into a visual narrative of the game. This process can test the refereeâ€™s impression, but it can also shape what appears on screen as â€œthe incident.â€</p> <p>A <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1186/s41235-018-0105-8\">study of 88 elite referees from five countries</a> found slow motion produced more severe sanctions than normal speed. An accurate slow-motion fragment can produce a contextually incomplete judgment when compared with the whole event.</p> <p>Consider two children, A and B, competing for a reward. A provokes B in ways a parent, C, doesnâ€™t see. When B pushes A, C sees only the reaction. B is still responsible, but the fuller context can change how the act is understood.</p> <p>This is the test we suggest: could an omitted interaction change perceptions about what offence occurred, who was responsible or what consequences should follow? By that measure, reviews should end once possession, position and opportunities to challenge have been genuinely reset.</p> <h2>Making reviews fairer</h2> <p>We propose three changes to make VAR reviews clearer and fairer. </p> <p>First, the refereeâ€™s initial call on the field, including any decision to let play continue, should establish what actually needs reviewing. Second, the replay should be checked against that specific call. Third, the human referee should interpret what the replay shows using the relevant passage of play, <a href=\"https://www.theifab.com/laws-of-the-game-documents/?language=all&amp;year=2026%2F27\">FIFAâ€™s official rules</a> and footballâ€™s shared norms for what counts as fair.</p> <p>Footballâ€™s law-making body <a href=\"https://www.theifab.com/laws/latest/video-assistant-referee-var-protocol/\">allows competitions to publish public VAR explanations</a>. To preserve match flow, FIFA should add a public post-match note for consequential reviews and checks, laying out the timing and distance of the incident, and the intervention threshold, including whether play continued or reset.</p> <p>Referees should also judge intensity at normal speed, and only use slow motion to confirm that contact happened, not to judge how hard it was. The technology should support human judgment, not replace it. The clarity of an isolated replay must never be mistaken for the completeness of a fair judgment, since only a human referee can supply that judgment.</p><img src=\"https://counter.theconversation.com/content/287676/count.gif\" alt=\"The Conversation\" width=\"1\" height=\"1\" /> <p class=\"fine-print\"><em><span>The authors do not work for, consult, own shares in or receive funding from any company or organisation that would benefit from this article, and have disclosed no relevant affiliations beyond their academic appointment.</span></em></p>"},{"kind":"producing-content.farm.research-packet.source","subject":"racism-at-the-world-cup-is-an-ugly-reality-how-to-understand-where-it-comes-from","wire":"decolonization","path":"urn:newscable-bundle:16c67ed98ed8fc4680d2e766cbf774765c029e15109a6c3a475c99e250408504","title":"Racism at the World Cup is an ugly reality. How to understand where it comes from","url":"https://theconversation.com/racism-at-the-world-cup-is-an-ugly-reality-how-to-understand-where-it-comes-from-287297","domain":"theconversation.com","published":"2026-07-16T14:28:15Z","assertedAt":"2026-07-19T15:04:15.228Z","score":30,"matchedTerms":["football","cup","final"],"summary":"<figure><img src=\"https://images.theconversation.com/files/748358/original/file-20260716-57-4yz928.jpg?ixlib=rb-4.1.0&amp;rect=0%2C1%2C6452%2C4301&amp;q=45&amp;auto=format&amp;w=1050&amp;h=700&amp;fit=crop\" /><figcaption><span class=\"caption\">Racism was in the news once again at the World Cup. But it comes in many forms.</span> <span class=\"attribution\"><a class=\"source\" href=\"https://www.pexels.com/photo/a-man-sitting-on-a-football-field-5247136/\">Thirdman/Pexels</a></span></figcaption></figure><p>No African teams made it past the quarter-finals of the 2026 menâ€™s football <a href=\"https://www.fifa.com/en/tournaments/mens/worldcup/canadamexicousa2026/standings\">World Cup</a>, but their strategic and tactical play left a <a href=\"https://novanews.co.za/african-teams-silence-critics-with-strong-world-cup-showing/#google_vignette\">lasting</a> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/shorts/eAF-HVdyWs0\">impression</a>. </p> <p>The success of African teams doesnâ€™t come out of the blue, itâ€™s the result of a long <a href=\"https://theconversation.com/world-cup-history-a-timeline-of-africas-greatest-moments-on-the-field-286908\">process</a>. Itâ€™s therefore surprising that African football remains underrated and downgraded by experts, often in a manner that reveals their ignorance both to African football and history. At times, their comments have a highly problematic racist tone.</p> <p>This was the case with former star player <a href=\"https://www.transfermarkt.co.za/bastian-schweinsteiger/profil/spieler/2514\">Bastian Schweinsteiger</a>, now a longtime commentator for a German TV network. Ahead of a group stage match against CÃ´te dâ€™Ivoire, he was asked what Germanyâ€™s team could expect. He <a href=\"https://www.espn.co.uk/football/story/_/id/49173319/jurgen-klopp-refuses-question-bastian-schweinsteiger-remarks-ivory-coast-play-style\">responded</a>:</p> <blockquote> <p>A bit African football, a bit unorthodox, a bit wild â€¦ perhaps also not so conditioned by tactics. We must be prepared for it to be unpredictable.</p> </blockquote> <p>Setting aside the fact that the German team was often the weaker side â€“ both tactically and in terms of style of play â€“ this characterisation is inappropriate and offensive. Itâ€™s based on a <a href=\"https://theconversation.com/racial-stereotypes-of-african-footballers-persist-a-world-cup-is-a-good-time-to-talk-about-them-286673\">stereotyped</a>, colonialist view of Africa.</p> <p></p> <p>On the one hand, Schweinsteiger <a href=\"https://www.dw.com/en/schweinsteigers-ivory-coast-comments-spark-racism-debate/a-77674794\">faced criticism that his statement was racist</a>. </p> <p>As Cote dâ€™Ivoireâ€™s manager, Emerse Fae, <a href=\"https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/articles/cx2x0ev2deqo\">said</a>:</p> <blockquote> <p>It is odd he would speak that way. We could call it racist â€¦ I can only hope it is a clumsy statement, rather than something going on in his mind.</p> </blockquote> <figure> <iframe width=\"440\" height=\"260\" src=\"https://www.youtube.com/embed/Kzgtcg8lREA?wmode=transparent&amp;start=0\" frameborder=\"0\" allowfullscreen=\"\"></iframe> </figure> <p>On the other hand, there were many people on online forums and <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DaLU8CfoRO8/\">social media</a> who wondered why Schweinsteigerâ€™s statement was considered racist, <a href=\"https://www.reuters.com/sports/soccer/schweinsteiger-rejects-racism-claims-broadcaster-ard-backs-pundit-2026-06-26/\">as did</a> his broadcaster, ARD. </p> <p>Schweinsteiger <a href=\"https://www.reuters.com/sports/soccer/schweinsteiger-rejects-racism-claims-broadcaster-ard-backs-pundit-2026-06-26/\">responded weakly</a> to the criticism:</p> <blockquote> <p>I was talking about football, not people. This is a football analysis â€“ nothing more, nothing less. </p> </blockquote> <p>I often hear this kind of downplaying when football in Africa and its people are subtly devalued. As an <a href=\"https://www.sobi.uni-passau.de/en/southeast-asian-studies/team/postdocs/dr-christian-ungruhe\">anthropologist</a>, my <a href=\"https://www.manchesterhive.com/display/9781526120274/9781526120274.xml?rskey=1NdWtS&amp;result=1&amp;print\">research</a> focuses on the migration of football players from various parts of Africa and on the forces that have shaped the evolution of African football, especially the modern game. A key <a href=\"https://brill.com/view/journals/afdi/6/2/article-p196_4.xml\">focus</a> is on racialisation, othering, and racism experienced by players. </p> <p>The <a href=\"https://www.manchesterhive.com/display/9781526120274/9781526120274.00015.xml\">research shows</a> that African footballers, and Black players more generally, face different forms of racism. </p> <p>On social media, they frequently encounter open and hostile rejection and insults. A recent example is the offensive post by Paraguayan senator Celeste Amarilla, after her countryâ€™s loss to France. She <a href=\"https://www.nytimes.com/athletic/7427643/2026/07/07/kylian-mbappe-france-paraguay-racism-senator-amarilla/\">described Kylian MbappÃ©</a> as a</p> <blockquote> <p>colonised Cameroonian, desperately trying to pass himself off as French â€¦ resentful, arrogant and ugly. </p> </blockquote> <p>Players from Africa also face <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.33196/juridikum202502020701\">structural disadvantages</a> in the football industry. This is evident in <a href=\"https://libra.unine.ch/server/api/core/bitstreams/28690f7b-85a1-46bb-89c0-3173d058fedc/content\">shorter contracts and lower salaries</a>, as well as <a href=\"https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/10.1177/10126902221081125\">peripheral playing positions</a> and less consideration for <a href=\"https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/full/10.1177/1012690216656807\">coaching and administrative roles</a>, as various studies show. </p> <p>After all, they are also constantly confronted with more subtle forms of everyday racism, <a href=\"https://www.nzz.ch/sport/mist-nun-geht-die-debatte-wieder-los-was-darf-man-denn-noch-sagen-dachte-der-afrika-experte-nach-schweinsteigers-aussagen-zum-wilden-fussball-ld.10013653\">which manifests</a> itself in their abilities and qualities being devalued. They are often reduced to their physical appearance, their intelligence denied.</p> <figure> <iframe width=\"440\" height=\"260\" src=\"https://www.youtube.com/embed/Hlz3HY2uZv4?wmode=transparent&amp;start=0\" frameborder=\"0\" allowfullscreen=\"\"></iframe> </figure> <p>Everyday racism is often expressed subtly and white people <a href=\"https://www.tagesspiegel.de/sport/rassistische-zuschreibungen-aus-der-kolonialzeit-wie-der-fussball-afrika-klischees-am-leben-halt-15761487.html\">like Schweinsteiger</a> are often not aware of its racist connotation. Therefore, <a href=\"https://www.spiegel.de/sport/vorurteile-im-fussball-die-wilden-waren-wir-deutschen-a-3824b4af-b986-48d8-85bc-88ddac218ce1\">the question</a> of whether Bastian Schweinsteiger is a racist or not is not the crucial one (he most likely is not).</p> <p>The key question is rather why these stereotypes are repeatedly expressed in football and why, for many people, they seem to reflect reality rather than appear problematic. To understand this, we need to do justice to Africaâ€™s diversity and further look at the continentâ€™s colonial history.</p> <h2>Colonial stereotypes</h2> <p>For one thing, simplistic statements like Schweinsteigerâ€™s disparage football across an entire continent without making any distinctions. There is just as little such thing as an <a href=\"https://www.moroccoworldnews.com/2026/06/325902/schweinsteigers-wild-african-football-remark-repeats-a-tired-stereotype/\">â€œAfricanâ€ style of football</a> as there is a â€œEuropeanâ€ one. This does not do justice to a complex reality and it reveals either ignorance or indifference. </p> <p>Second, describing African football as â€œunorthodox,â€ â€œwild,â€ and â€œnot characterised by tacticsâ€, <a href=\"https://www.reuters.com/sports/soccer/racist-commentary-about-black-players-comes-under-scrutiny-world-cup-2026-07-06/\">perpetuates stereotypes</a> from the colonial era that extend beyond the sport. My <a href=\"https://pure.eur.nl/ws/portalfiles/portal/47331921/ungruhe_fav_126788.pdf\">study</a> of African footballers in Germany shows how the players are vulnerable to broader, structural vulnerabilities in Europe.</p> <p>How many view African migrant footballers is grounded in colonial thinking. European colonisation legitimised itself by <a href=\"https://www.dandc.eu/en/article/oppressive-imperial-powers-claimed-be-spreading-civilisation-whilst-promoting-racism\">ranking people</a>. The enlightened European, capable of reflection, and the uneducated African, who must be tamed. </p> <p>Starting in the 1920s, colonial administrators deliberately <a href=\"https://phambo.wiser.org.za/files/seminars/Alegi2010.pdf\">used football to subjugate Africans</a>. Sport was used to teach team spirit and rules to the allegedly naÃ¯ve, raw and perhaps threatening African in order to turn him into a person loyal to the colony.</p> <p>Openly showing resistance was dangerous. But interestingly, this happened on the football field. Studies <a href=\"https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/africa/article/abs/kickin-it-leisure-politics-and-football-in-colonial-zanzibar-1900s1950s/A97494FF2D4FEB7BFA1252B4A11A6309\">outline</a> how some Africans didnâ€™t try to play by the rules. They tried to make feints, dribbles, and tricks â€“ on the one hand to entertain their own people, but also to display defiance. </p> <p>During the struggle for independence, football was then used by some African countries to <a href=\"https://www.jstor.org/stable/43305103?seq=1\">foster a sense</a> of national and pan-African unity. After all, colonial borders had been drawn arbitrarily. National football teams were important, but so was the style of play â€“ integrity and technical skill presented an alternative to the colonial image. </p> <p>Over the years, national playing styles have developed in various ways. They are influenced by cultural values, transnational linkages and the game becoming professionalised. However, the original mix â€“ powerful bodies, skillful dribbling, and a childlike, naÃ¯ve approach â€“ has become ingrained in European minds.</p> <h2>Slow poison</h2> <p>At first glance, subtle disparaging remarks like Schweinsteigerâ€™s may not seem to be as racist as the open insults and degrading comments seen every day in football and society. But they are hurtful. And because they are firmly embedded in the social knowledge of European countries, they are <a href=\"https://www.sueddeutsche.de/projekte/artikel/jetzt/tah-schweinsteiger-weltmeisterschaft-rassismus-e998278/?reduced=true\">hard to erase</a>. </p> <hr> <p> <em> <strong> Read more: <a href=\"https://theconversation.com/racial-stereotypes-of-african-footballers-persist-a-world-cup-is-a-good-time-to-talk-about-them-286673\">Racial stereotypes of African footballers persist. A World Cup is a good time to talk about them</a> </strong> </em> </p> <hr> <p>Rather, they are a creeping poison that, when constantly reproduced, shapes a societyâ€™s collective consciousness. As my research has <a href=\"https://brill.com/view/journals/afdi/6/2/article-p196_4.xml\">found</a>, such remarks not only disparage football in Africa but also shape the general perception of a continent that is allegedly backward at every level. </p> <p>To tackle the powerful colonial-era image of an African inferiority, it is important to take action against all forms of racist disparagement in football and beyond, whether they are unintentional, subtle, or explicit.</p><img src=\"https://counter.theconversation.com/content/287297/count.gif\" alt=\"The Conversation\" width=\"1\" height=\"1\" /> <p class=\"fine-print\"><em><span>Christian Ungruhe receives funding from the University of Passau. </span></em></p>"},{"kind":"producing-content.farm.research-packet.source","subject":"odyssey-to-africa-s-last-colony","wire":"decolonization","path":"urn:newscable-bundle:eef5fece67c050873dd582f9a7435270032041af1e2903157776626782ab3c63","title":"Odyssey to Africaâ€™s last colony","url":"https://africasacountry.com/2026/07/odyssey-to-africas-last-colony","domain":"africasacountry.com","published":"2026-07-17T15:16:23.012431Z","assertedAt":"2026-07-19T15:04:15.228Z","score":10,"matchedTerms":["cup"],"summary":"In the run-up to its theatrical release, Christopher Nolanâ€™s The Odyssey became embroiled in online polemics after Elon Musk attacked the movieâ€™s supposed â€œwokeâ€ casting. Yet beyond this contrived spectacle lies a far more important criticism to be made of the filmmakers: their decision to shoot part of the movie in Africaâ€™s last colony, Western Sahara. Enjoying generous subsidies from the Moroccan state, they lent legitimacy to its illegal occupation regime. As Nolan and his crew filmed along the coast around the port city of Dakhla last summer, an open letter condemning the move was signed by prominent figures in world cinema, including Javier Bardem, Pedro AlmodÃ³var, and Paul Laverty. â€œMr. Nolan filmed there without the consent of the Sahrawi people,â€ it read, in reference to the majority nationality in Western Sahara. â€œThe only consent he received came from the occupying force: Morocco.â€ In particular, Oscar-winning actor Bardem did not hold back. As he posted the letter on his Instagram account, he added: â€œFor 50 years, Morocco has occupied Western Sahara, expelling the Sahrawi people from their cities. Dakhla is one of them, converted by the Moroccan occupiers into a tourist destination and now a film set, always with the aim of erasing the Sahrawi identity of the city.â€ Covering an area the size of Britain, Western Sahara is designated as a â€œnon-self-governing territoryâ€ by the United Nations and remains on its official list of territories still awaiting decolonization. Retained by Francisco Francoâ€™s Spain even as other European colonies won their freedom, it did not gain independence even after the dictatorâ€™s demise in 1975. Instead, neighboring Morocco and Mauritania invaded â€” with at least 40 percent of the Sahrawi population at the time fleeing to neighboring Algeria to escape the Moroccan air forceâ€™s bombing campaign. Half a century later, 173,000 Sahrawis still remain in Algerian refugee camps. The native Sahrawis living under Moroccan occupation are subject to what Freedom House categorizes as among the least free political systems on the planet. The Odyssey is the first major Hollywood production to shoot scenes in Western Sahara. This would have been unthinkable prior to 2020, when President Donald Trump broke with decades of US foreign policy and recognized Moroccoâ€™s illegally established sovereignty over the territory. The decision was part of a quid pro quo in exchange for Moroccoâ€™s normalization of ties with Israel. Now Nolan and crewâ€™s four-day shoot in Dakhla illustrates how quickly Hollywood studios have moved to exploit the opportunities created by Trumpâ€™s transactional diplomacy. This subsidized occupation cinema is yet another symptom of the breakdown of whatever remained of a rules-based liberal order. For Moroccan officials, the presence of The Odyssey crew was a propaganda coup. They have made it clear they see it as just the beginning of Dakhlaâ€™s development as a base for international film productions. This is despite the fact that the wider Western Sahara remains the site of an ongoing armed conflict between the countryâ€™s military and the Sahrawi pro-independence movement, the Polisario Front. Last month, three Polisario fighters were killed in a Moroccan drone attack close to the 2,700-kilometer sand berm that separates Moroccan-controlled territory from the desert areas held by Polisario. If the construction of the vast defensive berm in the 1980s was Moroccoâ€™s attempt to entrench its military control over Western Sahara, the transformation of Dakhla into a tourist destination and green energy hub in recent years aims to consolidate the occupation as an irreversible economic reality. From the Moroccan leadershipâ€™s perspective, Sahrawi independence will look increasingly unrealistic if it can develop the territory in conjunction with international investors. It is also incentivizing Moroccan settlers to move to the territory with generous subsidies and jobs. The clearest expression of this strategy is Moroccoâ€™s Atlantic Initiative, which looks to provide landlocked countries in the Sahel region with maritime access to the Atlantic via a new â‚¬1.3 billion port facility currently under construction in Dakhla (and set to be operational by 2028). Positioning the occupied city as a key logistics hub for northwestern Africa, the project aims to further integrate Western Sahara into regional trade networks. Tourism is also key to normalizing Moroccan control, with Dakhla in particular recast as an international destination for kitesurfing and ecotourism. On a 2022 visit, Ivanka Trump and Jared Kushner were photographed at one of the growing number of high-end hotels on Dakhla peninsula, as well as on the sweeping Atlantic coastline that would later attract the film crew shooting The Odyssey . The coupleâ€™s holiday photo shoot offered a vision of luxury resorts and leisure in which the military occupation and indigenous Sahrawi population were erased from view. Airline Ryanairâ€™s announcement in 2024 that it was opening new direct routes connecting Spain with Dakhla and the Sahrawi capital El AaiÃºn marked a further expansion of this model of occupation tourism â€” even as the European Commission informed airlines that routes involving Western Sahara would not be covered by the terms of the EU-Moroccan aviation agreement. At the same time, international journalists, trade unionists, and human rights defenders have looked to break the Moroccan media blockade by boarding these low-cost flights over the last 18 months, only to be detained at the airport or arrested when they made contact with local Sahrawi activists. Footage from last year even showed a left-wing delegation from the European Parliament being physically blocked from disembarking a Ryanair flight by Moroccan security forces. The Odyssey â€™s subsidized production forms part of this same effort to establish Dakhla as an international â€” but firmly Moroccan â€” destination while limiting scrutiny of the occupation itself. Yet as news of the Dakhla shoot emerged last summer, Sahrawi filmmakers, journalists, and activists took to social media to contrast the free rein afforded to Nolanâ€™s Hollywood production with the systematic repression they faced in trying to document the Moroccan stateâ€™s human rights violations, or simply in exercising creative freedom. â€œI grew up in the Sahrawi refugee camps in Algeria and today, as a Sahrawi filmmaker from the occupied city of Dakhla, I cannot freely enter my homeland to tell my own stories,â€ director Brahim Chagaf posted as part of the online campaign organized by The Western Sahara International Film Festival. â€œThat is the great contradiction behind this landscape: while a few privileged people like Nolan can turn it into a movie, others are still waiting for the day when we can simply return to it.â€ Campaigner Ghalia Djimiâ€™s message was even starker: â€œMr Nolan: I am a human rights defender. Morocco disappeared me for 3 years and 7 months in a secret prison in occupied El AaiÃºn.â€ Her experience is far from exceptional. In its latest 2024 report, human rights NGO CODESA catalogued dozens of abuses carried out by Moroccan security forces that year. These included the repeated violent suppression of peaceful protests, the harassment and arbitrary detention of activists, and the suspicious deaths in custody of three Sahrawi civilians. In November 2023, the UN Working Group on Arbitrary Detention concluded that the detentions of two dozen Sahrawi activists and journalists, held ever since the Gdeim Izik protest camp in 2010, were illegal. It also found that in the case of 18 student activists detained in 2016, torture was used to extract confessions. As The Odyssey opens at the global box office, one of the Gdeim Izik prisoners, EnaÃ¢ma Asfari, is currently in the fourth week of an indefinite hunger strike. In calling for his immediate release last month, Frontline Defenders said it was â€œconcerned about reports describing medical neglect, reprisals and other forms of ill-treatment against Sahrawi human rights defenders in prison.â€ â€œWhen Christopher Nolan steps on the red carpet on his way to the premiereâ€™s screening, he will also be stepping on International Law,â€ insisted MarÃ­a CarriÃ³n, executive director of The Western Sahara International Film Festival, last month. â€œWe ask the public to treat this film as they would a movie made in occupied Ukraine with [Vladimir] Putinâ€™s permits, or in the illegal settlements in Palestine with [Benjamin] Netanyahuâ€™s blessing.â€ A series of rulings from the European Unionâ€™s highest court back this claim. Over the past decade, the Court of Justice of the European Union has repeatedly found that Western Sahara is a territory with a â€œseparate and distinctâ€ status to Morocco and that legally its resources cannot be exploited without the consent of the Sahrawi people. Those judgments concern specific EU-Moroccan trade agreements, which the EU has tried to revive despite successive rulings from its own courts that they contravened international law. But the rulings raise broader questions about the responsibilities of international companies operating in the occupied territory, including film studios. Given his back catalog, Nolanâ€™s brilliance as a writer and director is unquestionable. Yet with The Odyssey , he and Universal Pictures have set a dangerous precedent as they pioneered a new form of filmmaking for our Trumpian age: occupation cinema. A country subject to a brutal colonization and a system of effective apartheid is not a legitimate backdrop for either international tourism or a Hollywood blockbuster."}],"claims":[{"kind":"producing-content.farm.research-packet.claim","sourceSubject":"geometry-football-and-the-world-cup-final","sourceUrl":"https://theconversation.com/geometry-football-and-the-world-cup-final-287792","id":"title","predicate":"title","object":"Geometry, football and the World Cup final","force":"alleges","status":"supported","assertedAt":"2026-07-19T15:04:15.228Z"},{"kind":"producing-content.farm.research-packet.claim","sourceSubject":"geometry-football-and-the-world-cup-final","sourceUrl":"https://theconversation.com/geometry-football-and-the-world-cup-final-287792","id":"url","predicate":"url","object":"https://theconversation.com/geometry-football-and-the-world-cup-final-287792","force":"alleges","status":"supported","assertedAt":"2026-07-19T15:04:15.228Z"},{"kind":"producing-content.farm.research-packet.claim","sourceSubject":"geometry-football-and-the-world-cup-final","sourceUrl":"https://theconversation.com/geometry-football-and-the-world-cup-final-287792","id":"summary","predicate":"summary","object":"<p>Spain will play Argentina in the Fifa World Cup final on Sunday. The highly anticipated match takes place in New Jerseyâ€™s MetLife Stadium, with the teams vying for the most coveted prize in football.</p> <p>Attacking flair and physicality will no doubt play a role. But we could also see an underappreciated element of football come to the fore: <a href=\"https://theconversation.com/topics/geometry-8213\">geometry</a>.</p> <p>In their <a href=\"https://www.reuters.com/sports/soccer/spain-choke-life-out-france-reach-world-cup-final-2026-07-14/\">2-0 World Cup semi-final win over France</a>, Spain formed neat passing triangles around the French players. This sometimes made it look as if Spain had extra people on the field. </p> <p>Wherever a Spanish player received the ball, two teammates appeared at different angles â€“ with their French opponents seemingly always arriving a fraction too late.</p> <p>The triangle mattered not because it was neat, but because it gave Spain choice and France uncertainty. Each pass forced a French defender to commit to one possible future: press the ball, block the inside route, or protect the space behind. By the time that choice was made, the ball had often moved and the geometry had changed again.</p> <p>Spain were not simply finding space. They were controlling when and where the next space would appear.</p> <h2>Forcing choices</h2> <p>Put three attackers in a straight line and one defender can often block both supporting players. But move one supporting player sideways and the passing lines separate.</p> <p>The defender must choose which route to close. Press the ball and a pass can go around them. Protect the inside route and the outside may open. If another defender steps across, a Spanish player can attack the space left behind.</p> <p>A useful football triangle does not need equal sides or 60-degree angles. Its best shape changes with defendersâ€™ positions, body orientation, ball speed and the space available.</p> <p>A narrow triangle is easy to squeeze because one defender may block both lanes. A wide one makes the pass travel farther, giving an opponent more time to intercept it.</p> <p>In their semi-final match against France, Spain found the middle ground again and again â€“ close enough for quick combinations, but far enough apart to stretch Franceâ€™s press.</p> <h2>A pitch made of probabilities</h2> <p>Football geometry is a problem of time as well as space. A passing lane is not open simply because no defender is standing in it. The key question is whether the ball reaches the receiver before an opponent reaches the interception point. </p> <p>A diagram shows where players are â€“ but a useful model of the game asks where players can get to. <a href=\"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-021-97537-4\">Research using player-tracking data</a> estimates control of the pitch from playersâ€™ position, direction, speed and acceleration. The result is a constantly changing map of who is most likely to reach each area first. </p> <p>Pedro Porroâ€™s <a href=\"https://apnews.com/article/france-spain-world-cup-score-87fb7740fa552edf4bfd28d0e8727c23\">58th-minute semi-final goal</a> offered a compact example when, after exchanging passes with teammate Dani Olmo, he continued his run to score Spainâ€™s second. The first pass shifted the defendersâ€™ focus. Porroâ€™s movement created a new angle for the return.</p> <h2>A living network</h2> <p>Three-player combinations make this idea even harder to defend. Spainâ€™s strength was not just one triangle, but the speed with which several overlapped. A midfielder could be one corner of multiple shapes, connected to a defender, full-back, winger and another midfielder.</p> <p><a href=\"https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371%2Fjournal.pone.0171156\">Passing-network research</a> describes players as points and passes as links. Yet a final network diagram for a given football match misses something important: Spain were rebuilding those links with every movement and first touch they made. </p> <p>The value of a pass is not only where the ball goes, but how it changes what could happen next. That is why ten sideways passes are not automatically better than one pass that removes two defenders.</p> <h2>The shape that conquered basketball</h2> <p>Triangles are not unique to football. Under head coach Phil Jackson (1987-1998), US basketball team the Chicago Bulls made frequent use of a tactic called <a href=\"https://www.hoophall.com/hall-of-famers/tex-winter\">Tex Winterâ€™s triangle offence</a>. This places three players on one side of the court to create passing, cutting and scoring routes. </p> <p>The sport was different but the principle was the same: triangles preserve alternatives. Close one route and another remains; follow the ball and space opens elsewhere.</p> <p>In their match against Spain, Franceâ€™s problem was not simply a lack of running. Pressing works only when several defenders move together and close connected passing options. </p> <p>After the match, Kylian MbappÃ© said there had been <a href=\"https://www.reuters.com/sports/soccer/spain-choke-life-out-france-reach-world-cup-final-2026-07-14/\">â€œa lack of communicationâ€ in Franceâ€™s pressing</a>. Spain kept moving before France could reorganise.</p> <p>A triangle on a tactics board does nothing by itself. Its power depends on players scanning before receiving, opening their bodies, and using the first touch to preserve the option of more than one next action. </p> <p>The deeper contest was between Spainâ€™s speed of reconfiguration and Franceâ€™s speed of recognition. Spain kept changing the problem faster than France could solve it. The triangles were visible â€“ but their real advantage was control over what could happen next.</p> <p>Will we see a similar approach deployed in the final? Argentina will no doubt be closely studying their opponentâ€™s tactics from other matches. But geometry will still play a prominent role â€“ and may even prove a deciding factor.</p><img src=\"https://counter.theconversation.com/content/287792/count.gif\" alt=\"The Conversation\" width=\"1\" height=\"1\" /> <p class=\"fine-print\"><em><span>Conor Boland does not work for, consult, own shares in or receive funding from any company or organisation that would benefit from this article, and has disclosed no relevant affiliations beyond their academic appointment.</span></em></p>","force":"alleges","status":"supported","assertedAt":"2026-07-19T15:04:15.228Z"},{"kind":"producing-content.farm.research-packet.claim","sourceSubject":"geometry-football-and-the-world-cup-final","sourceUrl":"https://theconversation.com/geometry-football-and-the-world-cup-final-287792","id":"published","predicate":"published","object":"2026-07-17T14:08:05Z","force":"alleges","status":"supported","assertedAt":"2026-07-19T15:04:15.228Z"},{"kind":"producing-content.farm.research-packet.claim","sourceSubject":"what-cabo-verde-s-world-cup-debut-means-for-african-football","sourceUrl":"https://africasacountry.com/2026/07/what-cabo-verdes-world-cup-debut-means-for-african-football","id":"title","predicate":"title","object":"What Cabo Verdeâ€™s World Cup debut means for African football","force":"alleges","status":"supported","assertedAt":"2026-07-19T15:04:15.228Z"},{"kind":"producing-content.farm.research-packet.claim","sourceSubject":"what-cabo-verde-s-world-cup-debut-means-for-african-football","sourceUrl":"https://africasacountry.com/2026/07/what-cabo-verdes-world-cup-debut-means-for-african-football","id":"url","predicate":"url","object":"https://africasacountry.com/2026/07/what-cabo-verdes-world-cup-debut-means-for-african-football","force":"alleges","status":"supported","assertedAt":"2026-07-19T15:04:15.228Z"},{"kind":"producing-content.farm.research-packet.claim","sourceSubject":"what-cabo-verde-s-world-cup-debut-means-for-african-football","sourceUrl":"https://africasacountry.com/2026/07/what-cabo-verdes-world-cup-debut-means-for-african-football","id":"summary","predicate":"summary","object":"It happened. Cabo Verde lost to Argentina and exited the 2026 FIFA World Cup. And I couldnâ€™t be prouder. As a former football player, lifelong lover of the beautiful game, and Cabo Verdean-American, watching Cabo Verde compete on the world stage for the first time felt bigger than sports. What this team represented went far beyond the final score. At the start of the tournament, predictive models gave Cabo Verde almost no chance against former world champions Spain. The result: a stunning 0â€“0 draw anchored by seven saves from goalkeeper Josimar â€œVozinhaâ€ Dias. Against Uruguay, analysts dismissed Cabo Verdeâ€™s disciplined defensive strategy as no match for Marcelo Bielsaâ€™s aggressive attacking system. Commentators insisted Cabo Verde would never score. The result: a thrilling 2â€“2 draw, including the nationâ€™s first-ever World Cup goal from midfielder Kevin Pina. Then came another scoreless draw against Saudi Arabia, securing Cabo Verde a second-place group finish and a historic place as the smallest nation ever to reach the knockout rounds. By the time Cabo Verde faced defending champions Argentina, the world was paying attention and rooting for the underdogs. And for a moment, many of us believed the impossible could happen. When Deroy Duarte equalized to make it 1â€“1, we felt hope. When Sidny Lopes Cabral scored again to make it 2â€“2, belief swept across the world. Even after Argentina secured a 3â€“2 victory in extra time, Cabo Verde exited the tournament undefeated at the 90-minute mark against three former world champions. The loss hurt deeply. But not our pride. We may not have won on the scoreboard, but we won the respect of football fans around the world. In the days following the match, widely shared reports estimated that the Argentina versus Cabo Verde match attracted as many as 2.7 billion viewers worldwide. While those figures have not yet been officially verified by FIFA, the extraordinary claim reflected something that was unmistakably true: millions of people had fallen in love with Cabo Verdeâ€™s Cinderella story. For a nation of just over 500,000 people, that kind of global attention is extraordinary. Cabo Verde, an archipelago of 10 islands, was previously colonized by Portugal in the fifteenth century. It was a central port in the transatlantic slave trade before gaining independence in 1975. That independence came through the liberation struggle led by AmÃ­lcar Cabral, whose African Party for the Independence of Guinea and Cape Verde (PAIGC) fought Portuguese colonialism across both territories. Although Cabral was assassinated in 1973, two years before the independence, he helped make Cabo Verdeâ€™s independence possible, and remains one of Africaâ€™s most influential revolutionary thinkers. Today, the Cabo Verdean diaspora is estimated at roughly one million people, nearly double the resident population. More Cabo Verdeans now live in the United States, Portugal, the Netherlands, France, Brazil, Senegal, Angola, and elsewhere other than on the islands themselves. For decades, Cabo Verde existed on the margins of global football. The national team first entered FIFA World Cup qualification during the 2002 cycle and spent years competing against larger, wealthier, and better-funded football nations before finally qualifying for the 2026 tournament. The turning point came in the 2010s. The Blue Sharks qualified for their first Africa Cup of Nations in 2013 and stunned the continent by reaching the quarterfinals. Since then Cabo Verde has become regular AFCON competitors, and made their return to the quarterfinals in 2023. This was not a team built on global superstars or massive sponsorships. Many players came from lower-division clubs across Europe and the diaspora. Some were recruited through personal networks and online outreach, including LinkedIn, where the federation famously connected with Irish-based defender Roberto â€œPicoâ€ Lopes after he initially overlooked an earlier message written in Portuguese. What united the squad was discipline, camaraderie, and belief. Under manager Pedro â€œBubistaâ€ LeitÃ£o Brito, Cabo Verde played with tactical structure, composure, courage, and intelligence. In doing so, they challenged many of the assumptions often placed on African teams and Black athletes across sports: that they lack tactical discipline; that they rely more on physicality than strategy; or that they cannot compete at the highest level. Instead, Cabo Verde demonstrated that talent exists everywhere, even when visibility, infrastructure, and resources do not. One of the clearest examples of that visibility came through Vozinha. Before the World Cup, the 40-year-old goalkeeper was approaching retirement with a modest social media following of roughly 50,000 people. By the end of Cabo Verdeâ€™s historic run, he had become one of the breakout stars of the tournament and the most-followed goalkeeper in the world, with more than 26 million Instagram followers. Visibility changes careers. It creates sponsorship opportunities, attracts media attention, opens commercial doors, and reshapes how athletes, and the nations they represent, are perceived around the world. But this story is bigger than one player or one nation. The expanded World Cup also brought unprecedented visibility to African and Caribbean teams including Morocco, Senegal, Ghana, Egypt, Algeria, Tunisia, South Africa, DR Congo, CÃ´te dâ€™Ivoire, Haiti, and CuraÃ§ao. These nations were expected merely to make an appearance. Instead, they arrived prepared to compete. And that visibility matters. For a few extraordinary weeks, the world saw Cabo Verde not as a footnote, but as a nation worthy of respect, admiration, and serious attention. Together with the other African and Caribbean nations, they helped reshape the global conversation about the expanded 48-team tournament, the continued socio-political and economic realities facing emerging football nations, and the transformative power of access, opportunity, and visibility. In the words of Cabo Verde manager Bubista: â€œWe wanted to show that you can achieve great things regardless of your challenges, just as long as you have a dream and chase after it.â€ Thank you, Cabo Verde. And thank you to every African and Caribbean nation that stepped onto the world stage. Your fearlessness, resilience, and courage reminded the world that greatness has never been limited by geography. The future of African football is bright.","force":"alleges","status":"supported","assertedAt":"2026-07-19T15:04:15.228Z"},{"kind":"producing-content.farm.research-packet.claim","sourceSubject":"what-cabo-verde-s-world-cup-debut-means-for-african-football","sourceUrl":"https://africasacountry.com/2026/07/what-cabo-verdes-world-cup-debut-means-for-african-football","id":"published","predicate":"published","object":"2026-07-10T06:15:41.504236Z","force":"alleges","status":"supported","assertedAt":"2026-07-19T15:04:15.228Z"},{"kind":"producing-content.farm.research-packet.claim","sourceSubject":"tactically-complete-spain-vs-an-inspired-messi-led-argentina-who-will-win-the-world-cup-final","sourceUrl":"https://theconversation.com/tactically-complete-spain-vs-an-inspired-messi-led-argentina-who-will-win-the-world-cup-final-287372","id":"title","predicate":"title","object":"â€˜Tactically completeâ€™ Spain vs an inspired Messi-led Argentina. Who will win the World Cup final?","force":"alleges","status":"supported","assertedAt":"2026-07-19T15:04:15.228Z"},{"kind":"producing-content.farm.research-packet.claim","sourceSubject":"tactically-complete-spain-vs-an-inspired-messi-led-argentina-who-will-win-the-world-cup-final","sourceUrl":"https://theconversation.com/tactically-complete-spain-vs-an-inspired-messi-led-argentina-who-will-win-the-world-cup-final-287372","id":"url","predicate":"url","object":"https://theconversation.com/tactically-complete-spain-vs-an-inspired-messi-led-argentina-who-will-win-the-world-cup-final-287372","force":"alleges","status":"supported","assertedAt":"2026-07-19T15:04:15.228Z"},{"kind":"producing-content.farm.research-packet.claim","sourceSubject":"tactically-complete-spain-vs-an-inspired-messi-led-argentina-who-will-win-the-world-cup-final","sourceUrl":"https://theconversation.com/tactically-complete-spain-vs-an-inspired-messi-led-argentina-who-will-win-the-world-cup-final-287372","id":"summary","predicate":"summary","object":"<p>A record 102 matches have been played, leaving just two games to decide the <a href=\"https://theconversation.com/topics/fifa-world-cup-2026-186405\">2026 FIFA World Cup</a>: the third-place playoff (between France and England) and the final. </p> <p>The championship match, <a href=\"https://www.sbs.com.au/ondemand/collection/fifa-world-cup-2026-live-and-upcoming\">to be played on Sunday evening US time</a>, will see Spain take on Argentina in a fitting finale between <a href=\"https://inside.fifa.com/fifa-world-ranking/men\">the worldâ€™s two highest-ranked teams</a>. </p> <p>So, how did each team reach the final â€“ and how may it play out?</p> <p></p> <h2>How we got here</h2> <p>Both sides enter the final unbeaten after seven matches. </p> <p>Spain arrive as the reigning European champions and the worldâ€™s number two-ranked nation, while Argentina is the reigning Copa AmÃ©rica champion, defending World Cup holders and ranked first in the world.</p> <p>Spain are arguably <a href=\"https://www.nytimes.com/athletic/7446280/2026/07/15/spain-france-semifinal-tactical-analysis/\">the tournamentâ€™s most tactically complete side</a>. </p> <p>Luis de la Fuenteâ€™s team has been built on patient possession, relentless pressing and exceptional defensive organisation. </p> <p>They <a href=\"https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/articles/cpv3v0gkw9vo\">dismantled France 2-0 in the semi-final</a>, conceding very few clear chances, and have allowed just one goal throughout the tournament. In sports science terms, Spain have built towards peak performance for the final, after opening the tournament with a <a href=\"https://edition.cnn.com/2026/06/21/sport/spain-world-cup-ambitions-cape-verde\">draw against Cape Verde</a>. </p> <p>What has characterised the Spaniards is that no single player has stood above the rest â€“ the teamâ€™s success has been shared across the squad. </p> <p>This is a complete team coached by a manager at the peak of his powers. Six players have scored at least one goal, although Mikel Oyarzabal has scored five.</p> <p>Goalkeeper Unai SimÃ³n has been a rock and made some brilliant saves, and used intelligent positioning outside the box. Dani Olmo, despite being substituted twice, has been a constant attacking threat, while captain Rodri has also gone about his work with little fanfare, marshalling the team superbly from midfield. </p> <p></p> <h2>The Messi factor</h2> <p>Argentina is also a great team, but a different type of great team, and there is one standout player.</p> <p>There is little left to write about Lionel Messi that has not already been said. Yet the brilliance of this Argentine team is not simply built around his genius â€“ it is built around the extraordinary commitment of the ten players around him. </p> <hr> <p> <em> <strong> Read more: <a href=\"https://theconversation.com/messi-is-old-short-and-slow-how-is-he-still-dominating-at-the-world-cup-286238\">Messi is old, short and slow. How is he still dominating at the World Cup?</a> </strong> </em> </p> <hr> <p>They run for him, defend for him, sacrifice for him, and believe in him completely. </p> <p>Messi is afforded the freedom to conserve his energy, drifting into the spaces where he can do the greatest damage. His teammates carry the physical burden, allowing their captain to deliver moments of magic when they matter most. </p> <p>He is not merely Argentinaâ€™s leader â€“ he is their inspiration, their symbol, and the figure who carries the hopes of an entire nation. Throughout the tournament, whenever Argentina faced a moment of crisis, the players turned to Messi with the belief he would find a way.</p> <p>He nearly always does, and <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/football/2026/jul/16/england-appeared-to-have-lionel-messi-under-control-until-the-crucial-moments\">he did it again</a> in their unforgettable victory over England. </p> <p>That win represented more than qualification for another final. For many Argentinians, it carried the emotional weight of <a href=\"https://www.goal.com/en-au/lists/lionel-messi-argentina-england-diego-maradona-tribute-world-cup-semi-final/blt8cb51a8577016719\">Diego Maradonaâ€™s legacy</a> and offered the opportunity to defeat England on soccerâ€™s biggest stage through skill and determination rather than controversy. </p> <p>It was another defining chapter in Argentinaâ€™s remarkable World Cup story.</p> <p></p> <h2>How will the final play out?</h2> <p>Tactically, the match promises a fascinating contrast.</p> <p>Spain will seek to monopolise possession, patiently moving the ball until openings appear, while Argentina is comfortable defending compactly before striking quickly (usually through Messi).</p> <p>Whichever side controls the tempo in midfield is likely to have one hand on the trophy.</p> <p>Spain will try and play with the finesse they showed against the French, and Rodri will try to ensure they avoid a â€œstreet fightâ€, which the Argentinians may try and bait them into.</p> <p>History also adds another layer. Spain is chasing its second World Cup after the triumph in 2010, while Argentina is pursuing a fourth title and back-to-back World Cups â€“ an achievement not secured in more than six decades. </p> <p>The final therefore offers Spain the chance to cement a new era of dominance, while Argentina seek to extend one of the greatest periods in its history.</p> <p>It is a fitting climax to the tournament: the worldâ€™s number one-ranked team against the ranked team second. The defending champions against the European champions, and almost certainly Messiâ€™s final World Cup appearance.</p> <p></p> <p>In a sport now dominated by players who run all day and are increasingly similar, with an abundance of machine-like footballers and fewer players blessed with extraordinary skill and tactical understanding, this World Cup has produced some remarkable goals and performances.</p> <p>Harry Kane, Jude Bellingham, Erling Haaland and Kylian MbappÃ© have all produced moments of brilliance.</p> <p>But the final 25 minutes of the semi-final between England and Argentina, after England had taken a 1-0 lead, demonstrated that there has never been, and perhaps never will be, another player quite like Messi.</p> <p>We may be about to marvel at Messi for the last time. It is hard to believe he will not find a way to overcome this outstanding Spanish team.</p><img src=\"https://counter.theconversation.com/content/287372/count.gif\" alt=\"The Conversation\" width=\"1\" height=\"1\" /> <p class=\"fine-print\"><em><span>Steve Georgakis does not work for, consult, own shares in or receive funding from any company or organisation that would benefit from this article, and has disclosed no relevant affiliations beyond their academic appointment.</span></em></p>","force":"alleges","status":"supported","assertedAt":"2026-07-19T15:04:15.228Z"},{"kind":"producing-content.farm.research-packet.claim","sourceSubject":"tactically-complete-spain-vs-an-inspired-messi-led-argentina-who-will-win-the-world-cup-final","sourceUrl":"https://theconversation.com/tactically-complete-spain-vs-an-inspired-messi-led-argentina-who-will-win-the-world-cup-final-287372","id":"published","predicate":"published","object":"2026-07-17T02:59:08Z","force":"alleges","status":"supported","assertedAt":"2026-07-19T15:04:15.228Z"},{"kind":"producing-content.farm.research-packet.claim","sourceSubject":"world-cup-var-controversies-show-why-human-referees-should-decide-where-potential-fouls-begin","sourceUrl":"https://theconversation.com/world-cup-var-controversies-show-why-human-referees-should-decide-where-potential-fouls-begin-287676","id":"title","predicate":"title","object":"World Cup VAR controversies show why human referees should decide where potential fouls begin","force":"alleges","status":"supported","assertedAt":"2026-07-19T15:04:15.228Z"},{"kind":"producing-content.farm.research-packet.claim","sourceSubject":"world-cup-var-controversies-show-why-human-referees-should-decide-where-potential-fouls-begin","sourceUrl":"https://theconversation.com/world-cup-var-controversies-show-why-human-referees-should-decide-where-potential-fouls-begin-287676","id":"url","predicate":"url","object":"https://theconversation.com/world-cup-var-controversies-show-why-human-referees-should-decide-where-potential-fouls-begin-287676","force":"alleges","status":"supported","assertedAt":"2026-07-19T15:04:15.228Z"},{"kind":"producing-content.farm.research-packet.claim","sourceSubject":"world-cup-var-controversies-show-why-human-referees-should-decide-where-potential-fouls-begin","sourceUrl":"https://theconversation.com/world-cup-var-controversies-show-why-human-referees-should-decide-where-potential-fouls-begin-287676","id":"summary","predicate":"summary","object":"<p>Two contested decisions at the 2026 World Cup <a href=\"https://www.reuters.com/sports/soccer/vargentina-reach-semi-finals-under-cloud-complaints-2026-07-13/\">have reignited debate</a> over how video assistant referee (VAR) reviews should work, and how far back a referee should look when deciding what counts as part of the same passage of play.</p> <p>VAR allows referees to review contentious incidents using replay footage, <a href=\"https://www.theifab.com/laws/latest/video-assistant-referee-var-protocol/\">with the human referee making the final decision</a>. Itâ€™s meant to catch â€œclear and obvious errorsâ€ or incidents that the on-field officials missed. A referee reviewing an incident must decide how far back in time the reviewable passage of play extends.</p> <p>A 2021 <a href=\"https://fis.dshs-koeln.de/en/publications/video-assistant-referees-var-the-impact-of-technology-on-decision/\">study of 2,195 competitive football matches across 13 countries</a> found referees got calls right 92.1 per cent of the time, rising to 98.3 per cent after a VAR intervention. </p> <p>But replay only supplies the evidence. It doesnâ€™t supply interpretation, responsibility or a decision about how much of the play needs to be examined to establish appropriate context. That judgment falls to the referee. VARâ€™s effectiveness depends on referees interpreting the evidence within the incidentâ€™s interactions and its relevant context.</p> <p>As researchers <a href=\"https://digital.la84.org/digital/collection/p17103coll10/id/24101/\">who study the ethical implications of AI use in soccer</a>, weâ€™ve found that two decisions from the 2026 tournament illustrate how difficult these boundary calls can be in practice.</p> <h2>Argentinaâ€™s overturned goal</h2> <p>In the round of 16 match between Argentina and Egypt, VAR overturned an Egyptian goal after reviewing an incident that occurred nearly a minute earlier.</p> <p>Egypt was leading 1-0 when Mostafa Ziko scored in the 58th minute to make it 2-0. VAR recommended an on-field review of Egyptâ€™s Marwan Attia stepping on Argentinaâ€™s Lisandro MartÃ­nezâ€™s foot <a href=\"https://observer.co.uk/news/national/article/red-cards-var-disallowed-goals-conspiracy-theories-thrive-at-the-world-cup\">17 seconds before the goal</a> and more than 80 metres from where it was scored. </p> <p>The referee reviewed the incident and cancelled the goal. Argentina came back to win the game 3-2. </p> <p>The integral question is whether that foot-stepping incident still counted as being connected to the goal a minute later, or whether play had moved on enough to be treated as over and done with.</p> <p><a href=\"https://inside.fifa.com/refereeing/news/pierluigi-collina-we-are-not-influenced\">FIFA, soccerâ€™s governing body, says</a> an attacking phase in a game has no pre-set time or distance limit. </p> <p>Providing commentary on the situation, former referee <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/football/2026/jul/08/refereeing-world-cup-fine-input-tuchel-trump-comment\">Chris Foy argued the play should count as one continuous move</a> because the ball kept travelling forward. Another former referee, <a href=\"https://www.foxsports.com/stories/soccer/egypts-goal-vs-argentina-disallowed-after-var-check-not-why-var-brought-game\">Mark Clattenburg, disagreed</a>, arguing the distance covered and the time that had passed were enough to break the connection between the foul and the goal that followed.</p> <p>Egypt also appealed for a penalty late in the match, <a href=\"https://www.espn.com/soccer/story/_/id/49312344/pierluigi-collina-defends-egypt-argentina-referee-calls-send-home-world-cup\">after Mohamed Salah went down following contact with JuliÃ¡n Ãlvarez</a>, but it didnâ€™t prompt an on-field review; <a href=\"https://inside.fifa.com/refereeing/news/pierluigi-collina-we-are-not-influenced\">FIFA said</a> the defender played the ball first before normal contact followed. </p> <p>Both decisions may be legally defensible, but their different treatment shows why officials should explain where their review began, why the earlier act remained relevant and why one incident reached the VAR monitor while the other did not.</p> <h2>Haaland push overturns Norwayâ€™s goal</h2> <p>A second case, from Norwayâ€™s World Cup quarterfinal against England, raises a similar question: does a VAR review apply before the ball is back in play? </p> <p>TorbjÃ¸rn Heggem appeared to put Norway ahead in the 55th minute of the game, scoring on a rebound from a corner kick. </p> <p>But replays showed that as the corner was being delivered, Norwayâ€™s <a href=\"https://www.the-independent.com/sport/football/norway-england-goal-erling-haaland-foul-var-world-cup-b3013299.html\">Erling Haaland had extended both his arms and pushed Englandâ€™s Elliot Anderson to the ground</a>. The two had been <a href=\"https://www.si.com/soccer/how-new-world-cup-rule-england-vs-norway-world-cup-quarterfinal\">jostling for position</a> prior to the moment of contact, and Haaland appeared to be reacting to Anderson.</p> <p>The ball was not yet in play when the push occurred, but officials judged it had a direct causal link to the goal that followed: Haaland remained involved as the ball arrived from the corner kick, which fell to Patrick Berg. His shot was saved by goalkeeper Jordan Pickford, and the rebound fell to Heggem, who scored.</p> <p>Under a World Cup clarification, an attacking offence before a restart of play <a href=\"https://www.theifab.com/news/the-ifab-clarifies-var-protocol-for-attacking-team-offences-before-the-ball-is-in/\">may be reviewed if it directly affects a goal</a>. After review, the goal was cancelled and the corner retaken. Norway lost the match 2-1 after extra time.</p> <p>Questions were nonetheless asked about when the review began, since it did not appear to include Andersonâ€™s own physical duel with Haaland in the moments before the push. That earlier jostling does not necessarily mean Anderson provoked Haaland, but it is a reminder that the referee wasnâ€™t reviewing an isolated fact off a VAR screen. </p> <p>Neither case necessarily proves the refereesâ€™ decisions wrong, but both show that defining the relevant incident remains part of human refereeing.</p> <h2>Where does an incident begin?</h2> <p>VARâ€™s core problem is, in fact, defining the event under review: how far it extends through time and across the pitch, and where the causal connection ends. This requires reading whether ball possession, player positions and opportunities to challenge still carry the effects of an earlier incident, or whether the game has moved on and reset. </p> <p>Too narrow a frame isolates an action from the broader context that makes it significant. Too broad a frame drags in remote events after a new phase of the game has begun. </p> <p>Footage is not self-explanatory. Referees and video officials choose duration, angle, speed and relevant interactions, turning play into a visual narrative of the game. This process can test the refereeâ€™s impression, but it can also shape what appears on screen as â€œthe incident.â€</p> <p>A <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1186/s41235-018-0105-8\">study of 88 elite referees from five countries</a> found slow motion produced more severe sanctions than normal speed. An accurate slow-motion fragment can produce a contextually incomplete judgment when compared with the whole event.</p> <p>Consider two children, A and B, competing for a reward. A provokes B in ways a parent, C, doesnâ€™t see. When B pushes A, C sees only the reaction. B is still responsible, but the fuller context can change how the act is understood.</p> <p>This is the test we suggest: could an omitted interaction change perceptions about what offence occurred, who was responsible or what consequences should follow? By that measure, reviews should end once possession, position and opportunities to challenge have been genuinely reset.</p> <h2>Making reviews fairer</h2> <p>We propose three changes to make VAR reviews clearer and fairer. </p> <p>First, the refereeâ€™s initial call on the field, including any decision to let play continue, should establish what actually needs reviewing. Second, the replay should be checked against that specific call. Third, the human referee should interpret what the replay shows using the relevant passage of play, <a href=\"https://www.theifab.com/laws-of-the-game-documents/?language=all&amp;year=2026%2F27\">FIFAâ€™s official rules</a> and footballâ€™s shared norms for what counts as fair.</p> <p>Footballâ€™s law-making body <a href=\"https://www.theifab.com/laws/latest/video-assistant-referee-var-protocol/\">allows competitions to publish public VAR explanations</a>. To preserve match flow, FIFA should add a public post-match note for consequential reviews and checks, laying out the timing and distance of the incident, and the intervention threshold, including whether play continued or reset.</p> <p>Referees should also judge intensity at normal speed, and only use slow motion to confirm that contact happened, not to judge how hard it was. The technology should support human judgment, not replace it. The clarity of an isolated replay must never be mistaken for the completeness of a fair judgment, since only a human referee can supply that judgment.</p><img src=\"https://counter.theconversation.com/content/287676/count.gif\" alt=\"The Conversation\" width=\"1\" height=\"1\" /> <p class=\"fine-print\"><em><span>The authors do not work for, consult, own shares in or receive funding from any company or organisation that would benefit from this article, and have disclosed no relevant affiliations beyond their academic appointment.</span></em></p>","force":"alleges","status":"supported","assertedAt":"2026-07-19T15:04:15.228Z"},{"kind":"producing-content.farm.research-packet.claim","sourceSubject":"world-cup-var-controversies-show-why-human-referees-should-decide-where-potential-fouls-begin","sourceUrl":"https://theconversation.com/world-cup-var-controversies-show-why-human-referees-should-decide-where-potential-fouls-begin-287676","id":"published","predicate":"published","object":"2026-07-16T21:22:02Z","force":"alleges","status":"supported","assertedAt":"2026-07-19T15:04:15.228Z"},{"kind":"producing-content.farm.research-packet.claim","sourceSubject":"racism-at-the-world-cup-is-an-ugly-reality-how-to-understand-where-it-comes-from","sourceUrl":"https://theconversation.com/racism-at-the-world-cup-is-an-ugly-reality-how-to-understand-where-it-comes-from-287297","id":"title","predicate":"title","object":"Racism at the World Cup is an ugly reality. How to understand where it comes from","force":"alleges","status":"supported","assertedAt":"2026-07-19T15:04:15.228Z"},{"kind":"producing-content.farm.research-packet.claim","sourceSubject":"racism-at-the-world-cup-is-an-ugly-reality-how-to-understand-where-it-comes-from","sourceUrl":"https://theconversation.com/racism-at-the-world-cup-is-an-ugly-reality-how-to-understand-where-it-comes-from-287297","id":"url","predicate":"url","object":"https://theconversation.com/racism-at-the-world-cup-is-an-ugly-reality-how-to-understand-where-it-comes-from-287297","force":"alleges","status":"supported","assertedAt":"2026-07-19T15:04:15.228Z"},{"kind":"producing-content.farm.research-packet.claim","sourceSubject":"racism-at-the-world-cup-is-an-ugly-reality-how-to-understand-where-it-comes-from","sourceUrl":"https://theconversation.com/racism-at-the-world-cup-is-an-ugly-reality-how-to-understand-where-it-comes-from-287297","id":"summary","predicate":"summary","object":"<figure><img src=\"https://images.theconversation.com/files/748358/original/file-20260716-57-4yz928.jpg?ixlib=rb-4.1.0&amp;rect=0%2C1%2C6452%2C4301&amp;q=45&amp;auto=format&amp;w=1050&amp;h=700&amp;fit=crop\" /><figcaption><span class=\"caption\">Racism was in the news once again at the World Cup. But it comes in many forms.</span> <span class=\"attribution\"><a class=\"source\" href=\"https://www.pexels.com/photo/a-man-sitting-on-a-football-field-5247136/\">Thirdman/Pexels</a></span></figcaption></figure><p>No African teams made it past the quarter-finals of the 2026 menâ€™s football <a href=\"https://www.fifa.com/en/tournaments/mens/worldcup/canadamexicousa2026/standings\">World Cup</a>, but their strategic and tactical play left a <a href=\"https://novanews.co.za/african-teams-silence-critics-with-strong-world-cup-showing/#google_vignette\">lasting</a> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/shorts/eAF-HVdyWs0\">impression</a>. </p> <p>The success of African teams doesnâ€™t come out of the blue, itâ€™s the result of a long <a href=\"https://theconversation.com/world-cup-history-a-timeline-of-africas-greatest-moments-on-the-field-286908\">process</a>. Itâ€™s therefore surprising that African football remains underrated and downgraded by experts, often in a manner that reveals their ignorance both to African football and history. At times, their comments have a highly problematic racist tone.</p> <p>This was the case with former star player <a href=\"https://www.transfermarkt.co.za/bastian-schweinsteiger/profil/spieler/2514\">Bastian Schweinsteiger</a>, now a longtime commentator for a German TV network. Ahead of a group stage match against CÃ´te dâ€™Ivoire, he was asked what Germanyâ€™s team could expect. He <a href=\"https://www.espn.co.uk/football/story/_/id/49173319/jurgen-klopp-refuses-question-bastian-schweinsteiger-remarks-ivory-coast-play-style\">responded</a>:</p> <blockquote> <p>A bit African football, a bit unorthodox, a bit wild â€¦ perhaps also not so conditioned by tactics. We must be prepared for it to be unpredictable.</p> </blockquote> <p>Setting aside the fact that the German team was often the weaker side â€“ both tactically and in terms of style of play â€“ this characterisation is inappropriate and offensive. Itâ€™s based on a <a href=\"https://theconversation.com/racial-stereotypes-of-african-footballers-persist-a-world-cup-is-a-good-time-to-talk-about-them-286673\">stereotyped</a>, colonialist view of Africa.</p> <p></p> <p>On the one hand, Schweinsteiger <a href=\"https://www.dw.com/en/schweinsteigers-ivory-coast-comments-spark-racism-debate/a-77674794\">faced criticism that his statement was racist</a>. </p> <p>As Cote dâ€™Ivoireâ€™s manager, Emerse Fae, <a href=\"https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/articles/cx2x0ev2deqo\">said</a>:</p> <blockquote> <p>It is odd he would speak that way. We could call it racist â€¦ I can only hope it is a clumsy statement, rather than something going on in his mind.</p> </blockquote> <figure> <iframe width=\"440\" height=\"260\" src=\"https://www.youtube.com/embed/Kzgtcg8lREA?wmode=transparent&amp;start=0\" frameborder=\"0\" allowfullscreen=\"\"></iframe> </figure> <p>On the other hand, there were many people on online forums and <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DaLU8CfoRO8/\">social media</a> who wondered why Schweinsteigerâ€™s statement was considered racist, <a href=\"https://www.reuters.com/sports/soccer/schweinsteiger-rejects-racism-claims-broadcaster-ard-backs-pundit-2026-06-26/\">as did</a> his broadcaster, ARD. </p> <p>Schweinsteiger <a href=\"https://www.reuters.com/sports/soccer/schweinsteiger-rejects-racism-claims-broadcaster-ard-backs-pundit-2026-06-26/\">responded weakly</a> to the criticism:</p> <blockquote> <p>I was talking about football, not people. This is a football analysis â€“ nothing more, nothing less. </p> </blockquote> <p>I often hear this kind of downplaying when football in Africa and its people are subtly devalued. As an <a href=\"https://www.sobi.uni-passau.de/en/southeast-asian-studies/team/postdocs/dr-christian-ungruhe\">anthropologist</a>, my <a href=\"https://www.manchesterhive.com/display/9781526120274/9781526120274.xml?rskey=1NdWtS&amp;result=1&amp;print\">research</a> focuses on the migration of football players from various parts of Africa and on the forces that have shaped the evolution of African football, especially the modern game. A key <a href=\"https://brill.com/view/journals/afdi/6/2/article-p196_4.xml\">focus</a> is on racialisation, othering, and racism experienced by players. </p> <p>The <a href=\"https://www.manchesterhive.com/display/9781526120274/9781526120274.00015.xml\">research shows</a> that African footballers, and Black players more generally, face different forms of racism. </p> <p>On social media, they frequently encounter open and hostile rejection and insults. A recent example is the offensive post by Paraguayan senator Celeste Amarilla, after her countryâ€™s loss to France. She <a href=\"https://www.nytimes.com/athletic/7427643/2026/07/07/kylian-mbappe-france-paraguay-racism-senator-amarilla/\">described Kylian MbappÃ©</a> as a</p> <blockquote> <p>colonised Cameroonian, desperately trying to pass himself off as French â€¦ resentful, arrogant and ugly. </p> </blockquote> <p>Players from Africa also face <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.33196/juridikum202502020701\">structural disadvantages</a> in the football industry. This is evident in <a href=\"https://libra.unine.ch/server/api/core/bitstreams/28690f7b-85a1-46bb-89c0-3173d058fedc/content\">shorter contracts and lower salaries</a>, as well as <a href=\"https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/10.1177/10126902221081125\">peripheral playing positions</a> and less consideration for <a href=\"https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/full/10.1177/1012690216656807\">coaching and administrative roles</a>, as various studies show. </p> <p>After all, they are also constantly confronted with more subtle forms of everyday racism, <a href=\"https://www.nzz.ch/sport/mist-nun-geht-die-debatte-wieder-los-was-darf-man-denn-noch-sagen-dachte-der-afrika-experte-nach-schweinsteigers-aussagen-zum-wilden-fussball-ld.10013653\">which manifests</a> itself in their abilities and qualities being devalued. They are often reduced to their physical appearance, their intelligence denied.</p> <figure> <iframe width=\"440\" height=\"260\" src=\"https://www.youtube.com/embed/Hlz3HY2uZv4?wmode=transparent&amp;start=0\" frameborder=\"0\" allowfullscreen=\"\"></iframe> </figure> <p>Everyday racism is often expressed subtly and white people <a href=\"https://www.tagesspiegel.de/sport/rassistische-zuschreibungen-aus-der-kolonialzeit-wie-der-fussball-afrika-klischees-am-leben-halt-15761487.html\">like Schweinsteiger</a> are often not aware of its racist connotation. Therefore, <a href=\"https://www.spiegel.de/sport/vorurteile-im-fussball-die-wilden-waren-wir-deutschen-a-3824b4af-b986-48d8-85bc-88ddac218ce1\">the question</a> of whether Bastian Schweinsteiger is a racist or not is not the crucial one (he most likely is not).</p> <p>The key question is rather why these stereotypes are repeatedly expressed in football and why, for many people, they seem to reflect reality rather than appear problematic. To understand this, we need to do justice to Africaâ€™s diversity and further look at the continentâ€™s colonial history.</p> <h2>Colonial stereotypes</h2> <p>For one thing, simplistic statements like Schweinsteigerâ€™s disparage football across an entire continent without making any distinctions. There is just as little such thing as an <a href=\"https://www.moroccoworldnews.com/2026/06/325902/schweinsteigers-wild-african-football-remark-repeats-a-tired-stereotype/\">â€œAfricanâ€ style of football</a> as there is a â€œEuropeanâ€ one. This does not do justice to a complex reality and it reveals either ignorance or indifference. </p> <p>Second, describing African football as â€œunorthodox,â€ â€œwild,â€ and â€œnot characterised by tacticsâ€, <a href=\"https://www.reuters.com/sports/soccer/racist-commentary-about-black-players-comes-under-scrutiny-world-cup-2026-07-06/\">perpetuates stereotypes</a> from the colonial era that extend beyond the sport. My <a href=\"https://pure.eur.nl/ws/portalfiles/portal/47331921/ungruhe_fav_126788.pdf\">study</a> of African footballers in Germany shows how the players are vulnerable to broader, structural vulnerabilities in Europe.</p> <p>How many view African migrant footballers is grounded in colonial thinking. European colonisation legitimised itself by <a href=\"https://www.dandc.eu/en/article/oppressive-imperial-powers-claimed-be-spreading-civilisation-whilst-promoting-racism\">ranking people</a>. The enlightened European, capable of reflection, and the uneducated African, who must be tamed. </p> <p>Starting in the 1920s, colonial administrators deliberately <a href=\"https://phambo.wiser.org.za/files/seminars/Alegi2010.pdf\">used football to subjugate Africans</a>. Sport was used to teach team spirit and rules to the allegedly naÃ¯ve, raw and perhaps threatening African in order to turn him into a person loyal to the colony.</p> <p>Openly showing resistance was dangerous. But interestingly, this happened on the football field. Studies <a href=\"https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/africa/article/abs/kickin-it-leisure-politics-and-football-in-colonial-zanzibar-1900s1950s/A97494FF2D4FEB7BFA1252B4A11A6309\">outline</a> how some Africans didnâ€™t try to play by the rules. They tried to make feints, dribbles, and tricks â€“ on the one hand to entertain their own people, but also to display defiance. </p> <p>During the struggle for independence, football was then used by some African countries to <a href=\"https://www.jstor.org/stable/43305103?seq=1\">foster a sense</a> of national and pan-African unity. After all, colonial borders had been drawn arbitrarily. National football teams were important, but so was the style of play â€“ integrity and technical skill presented an alternative to the colonial image. </p> <p>Over the years, national playing styles have developed in various ways. They are influenced by cultural values, transnational linkages and the game becoming professionalised. However, the original mix â€“ powerful bodies, skillful dribbling, and a childlike, naÃ¯ve approach â€“ has become ingrained in European minds.</p> <h2>Slow poison</h2> <p>At first glance, subtle disparaging remarks like Schweinsteigerâ€™s may not seem to be as racist as the open insults and degrading comments seen every day in football and society. But they are hurtful. And because they are firmly embedded in the social knowledge of European countries, they are <a href=\"https://www.sueddeutsche.de/projekte/artikel/jetzt/tah-schweinsteiger-weltmeisterschaft-rassismus-e998278/?reduced=true\">hard to erase</a>. </p> <hr> <p> <em> <strong> Read more: <a href=\"https://theconversation.com/racial-stereotypes-of-african-footballers-persist-a-world-cup-is-a-good-time-to-talk-about-them-286673\">Racial stereotypes of African footballers persist. A World Cup is a good time to talk about them</a> </strong> </em> </p> <hr> <p>Rather, they are a creeping poison that, when constantly reproduced, shapes a societyâ€™s collective consciousness. As my research has <a href=\"https://brill.com/view/journals/afdi/6/2/article-p196_4.xml\">found</a>, such remarks not only disparage football in Africa but also shape the general perception of a continent that is allegedly backward at every level. </p> <p>To tackle the powerful colonial-era image of an African inferiority, it is important to take action against all forms of racist disparagement in football and beyond, whether they are unintentional, subtle, or explicit.</p><img src=\"https://counter.theconversation.com/content/287297/count.gif\" alt=\"The Conversation\" width=\"1\" height=\"1\" /> <p class=\"fine-print\"><em><span>Christian Ungruhe receives funding from the University of Passau. </span></em></p>","force":"alleges","status":"supported","assertedAt":"2026-07-19T15:04:15.228Z"},{"kind":"producing-content.farm.research-packet.claim","sourceSubject":"racism-at-the-world-cup-is-an-ugly-reality-how-to-understand-where-it-comes-from","sourceUrl":"https://theconversation.com/racism-at-the-world-cup-is-an-ugly-reality-how-to-understand-where-it-comes-from-287297","id":"published","predicate":"published","object":"2026-07-16T14:28:15Z","force":"alleges","status":"supported","assertedAt":"2026-07-19T15:04:15.228Z"},{"kind":"producing-content.farm.research-packet.claim","sourceSubject":"odyssey-to-africa-s-last-colony","sourceUrl":"https://africasacountry.com/2026/07/odyssey-to-africas-last-colony","id":"title","predicate":"title","object":"Odyssey to Africaâ€™s last colony","force":"alleges","status":"supported","assertedAt":"2026-07-19T15:04:15.228Z"},{"kind":"producing-content.farm.research-packet.claim","sourceSubject":"odyssey-to-africa-s-last-colony","sourceUrl":"https://africasacountry.com/2026/07/odyssey-to-africas-last-colony","id":"url","predicate":"url","object":"https://africasacountry.com/2026/07/odyssey-to-africas-last-colony","force":"alleges","status":"supported","assertedAt":"2026-07-19T15:04:15.228Z"},{"kind":"producing-content.farm.research-packet.claim","sourceSubject":"odyssey-to-africa-s-last-colony","sourceUrl":"https://africasacountry.com/2026/07/odyssey-to-africas-last-colony","id":"summary","predicate":"summary","object":"In the run-up to its theatrical release, Christopher Nolanâ€™s The Odyssey became embroiled in online polemics after Elon Musk attacked the movieâ€™s supposed â€œwokeâ€ casting. Yet beyond this contrived spectacle lies a far more important criticism to be made of the filmmakers: their decision to shoot part of the movie in Africaâ€™s last colony, Western Sahara. Enjoying generous subsidies from the Moroccan state, they lent legitimacy to its illegal occupation regime. As Nolan and his crew filmed along the coast around the port city of Dakhla last summer, an open letter condemning the move was signed by prominent figures in world cinema, including Javier Bardem, Pedro AlmodÃ³var, and Paul Laverty. â€œMr. Nolan filmed there without the consent of the Sahrawi people,â€ it read, in reference to the majority nationality in Western Sahara. â€œThe only consent he received came from the occupying force: Morocco.â€ In particular, Oscar-winning actor Bardem did not hold back. As he posted the letter on his Instagram account, he added: â€œFor 50 years, Morocco has occupied Western Sahara, expelling the Sahrawi people from their cities. Dakhla is one of them, converted by the Moroccan occupiers into a tourist destination and now a film set, always with the aim of erasing the Sahrawi identity of the city.â€ Covering an area the size of Britain, Western Sahara is designated as a â€œnon-self-governing territoryâ€ by the United Nations and remains on its official list of territories still awaiting decolonization. Retained by Francisco Francoâ€™s Spain even as other European colonies won their freedom, it did not gain independence even after the dictatorâ€™s demise in 1975. Instead, neighboring Morocco and Mauritania invaded â€” with at least 40 percent of the Sahrawi population at the time fleeing to neighboring Algeria to escape the Moroccan air forceâ€™s bombing campaign. Half a century later, 173,000 Sahrawis still remain in Algerian refugee camps. The native Sahrawis living under Moroccan occupation are subject to what Freedom House categorizes as among the least free political systems on the planet. The Odyssey is the first major Hollywood production to shoot scenes in Western Sahara. This would have been unthinkable prior to 2020, when President Donald Trump broke with decades of US foreign policy and recognized Moroccoâ€™s illegally established sovereignty over the territory. The decision was part of a quid pro quo in exchange for Moroccoâ€™s normalization of ties with Israel. Now Nolan and crewâ€™s four-day shoot in Dakhla illustrates how quickly Hollywood studios have moved to exploit the opportunities created by Trumpâ€™s transactional diplomacy. This subsidized occupation cinema is yet another symptom of the breakdown of whatever remained of a rules-based liberal order. For Moroccan officials, the presence of The Odyssey crew was a propaganda coup. They have made it clear they see it as just the beginning of Dakhlaâ€™s development as a base for international film productions. This is despite the fact that the wider Western Sahara remains the site of an ongoing armed conflict between the countryâ€™s military and the Sahrawi pro-independence movement, the Polisario Front. Last month, three Polisario fighters were killed in a Moroccan drone attack close to the 2,700-kilometer sand berm that separates Moroccan-controlled territory from the desert areas held by Polisario. If the construction of the vast defensive berm in the 1980s was Moroccoâ€™s attempt to entrench its military control over Western Sahara, the transformation of Dakhla into a tourist destination and green energy hub in recent years aims to consolidate the occupation as an irreversible economic reality. From the Moroccan leadershipâ€™s perspective, Sahrawi independence will look increasingly unrealistic if it can develop the territory in conjunction with international investors. It is also incentivizing Moroccan settlers to move to the territory with generous subsidies and jobs. The clearest expression of this strategy is Moroccoâ€™s Atlantic Initiative, which looks to provide landlocked countries in the Sahel region with maritime access to the Atlantic via a new â‚¬1.3 billion port facility currently under construction in Dakhla (and set to be operational by 2028). Positioning the occupied city as a key logistics hub for northwestern Africa, the project aims to further integrate Western Sahara into regional trade networks. Tourism is also key to normalizing Moroccan control, with Dakhla in particular recast as an international destination for kitesurfing and ecotourism. On a 2022 visit, Ivanka Trump and Jared Kushner were photographed at one of the growing number of high-end hotels on Dakhla peninsula, as well as on the sweeping Atlantic coastline that would later attract the film crew shooting The Odyssey . The coupleâ€™s holiday photo shoot offered a vision of luxury resorts and leisure in which the military occupation and indigenous Sahrawi population were erased from view. Airline Ryanairâ€™s announcement in 2024 that it was opening new direct routes connecting Spain with Dakhla and the Sahrawi capital El AaiÃºn marked a further expansion of this model of occupation tourism â€” even as the European Commission informed airlines that routes involving Western Sahara would not be covered by the terms of the EU-Moroccan aviation agreement. At the same time, international journalists, trade unionists, and human rights defenders have looked to break the Moroccan media blockade by boarding these low-cost flights over the last 18 months, only to be detained at the airport or arrested when they made contact with local Sahrawi activists. Footage from last year even showed a left-wing delegation from the European Parliament being physically blocked from disembarking a Ryanair flight by Moroccan security forces. The Odyssey â€™s subsidized production forms part of this same effort to establish Dakhla as an international â€” but firmly Moroccan â€” destination while limiting scrutiny of the occupation itself. Yet as news of the Dakhla shoot emerged last summer, Sahrawi filmmakers, journalists, and activists took to social media to contrast the free rein afforded to Nolanâ€™s Hollywood production with the systematic repression they faced in trying to document the Moroccan stateâ€™s human rights violations, or simply in exercising creative freedom. â€œI grew up in the Sahrawi refugee camps in Algeria and today, as a Sahrawi filmmaker from the occupied city of Dakhla, I cannot freely enter my homeland to tell my own stories,â€ director Brahim Chagaf posted as part of the online campaign organized by The Western Sahara International Film Festival. â€œThat is the great contradiction behind this landscape: while a few privileged people like Nolan can turn it into a movie, others are still waiting for the day when we can simply return to it.â€ Campaigner Ghalia Djimiâ€™s message was even starker: â€œMr Nolan: I am a human rights defender. Morocco disappeared me for 3 years and 7 months in a secret prison in occupied El AaiÃºn.â€ Her experience is far from exceptional. In its latest 2024 report, human rights NGO CODESA catalogued dozens of abuses carried out by Moroccan security forces that year. These included the repeated violent suppression of peaceful protests, the harassment and arbitrary detention of activists, and the suspicious deaths in custody of three Sahrawi civilians. In November 2023, the UN Working Group on Arbitrary Detention concluded that the detentions of two dozen Sahrawi activists and journalists, held ever since the Gdeim Izik protest camp in 2010, were illegal. It also found that in the case of 18 student activists detained in 2016, torture was used to extract confessions. As The Odyssey opens at the global box office, one of the Gdeim Izik prisoners, EnaÃ¢ma Asfari, is currently in the fourth week of an indefinite hunger strike. In calling for his immediate release last month, Frontline Defenders said it was â€œconcerned about reports describing medical neglect, reprisals and other forms of ill-treatment against Sahrawi human rights defenders in prison.â€ â€œWhen Christopher Nolan steps on the red carpet on his way to the premiereâ€™s screening, he will also be stepping on International Law,â€ insisted MarÃ­a CarriÃ³n, executive director of The Western Sahara International Film Festival, last month. â€œWe ask the public to treat this film as they would a movie made in occupied Ukraine with [Vladimir] Putinâ€™s permits, or in the illegal settlements in Palestine with [Benjamin] Netanyahuâ€™s blessing.â€ A series of rulings from the European Unionâ€™s highest court back this claim. Over the past decade, the Court of Justice of the European Union has repeatedly found that Western Sahara is a territory with a â€œseparate and distinctâ€ status to Morocco and that legally its resources cannot be exploited without the consent of the Sahrawi people. Those judgments concern specific EU-Moroccan trade agreements, which the EU has tried to revive despite successive rulings from its own courts that they contravened international law. But the rulings raise broader questions about the responsibilities of international companies operating in the occupied territory, including film studios. Given his back catalog, Nolanâ€™s brilliance as a writer and director is unquestionable. Yet with The Odyssey , he and Universal Pictures have set a dangerous precedent as they pioneered a new form of filmmaking for our Trumpian age: occupation cinema. A country subject to a brutal colonization and a system of effective apartheid is not a legitimate backdrop for either international tourism or a Hollywood blockbuster.","force":"alleges","status":"supported","assertedAt":"2026-07-19T15:04:15.228Z"},{"kind":"producing-content.farm.research-packet.claim","sourceSubject":"odyssey-to-africa-s-last-colony","sourceUrl":"https://africasacountry.com/2026/07/odyssey-to-africas-last-colony","id":"published","predicate":"published","object":"2026-07-17T15:16:23.012431Z","force":"alleges","status":"supported","assertedAt":"2026-07-19T15:04:15.228Z"}],"warrant":{"status":"research-intake","supportClaimCount":24,"refutationClaimCount":0,"uncertainClaimCount":0,"sourceDomainCount":2,"sourceDomains":["theconversation.com","africasacountry.com"],"note":"Current-provider claims are source-bound research intake. Prior-corpus excerpts are context only and license no public factual claim."},"editorialRisks":[{"code":"demand-is-not-evidence","severity":"note","message":"The assignment and search terms select attention but license no factual claim."},{"code":"prior-research-is-context-only","severity":"note","message":"Recovered prior research may guide reporting but is not promoted into source claims by this service."},{"code":"prior-research-gaps","severity":"warning","message":"Corpus Management reported 5 explicit recovery gaps; do not infer that omitted or unmatched material is absent from the wider corpus."}],"sourceUrls":["https://theconversation.com/geometry-football-and-the-world-cup-final-287792","https://africasacountry.com/2026/07/what-cabo-verdes-world-cup-debut-means-for-african-football","https://theconversation.com/tactically-complete-spain-vs-an-inspired-messi-led-argentina-who-will-win-the-world-cup-final-287372","https://theconversation.com/world-cup-var-controversies-show-why-human-referees-should-decide-where-potential-fouls-begin-287676","https://theconversation.com/racism-at-the-world-cup-is-an-ugly-reality-how-to-understand-where-it-comes-from-287297","https://africasacountry.com/2026/07/odyssey-to-africas-last-colony"],"candidateReferences":[{"id":"source-fff54bf73f2d","type":"webpage","title":"Geometry, football and the World Cup final","URL":"https://theconversation.com/geometry-football-and-the-world-cup-final-287792","container-title":"theconversation.com"},{"id":"source-09d02aa852e6","type":"webpage","title":"What Cabo Verdeâ€™s World Cup debut means for African football","URL":"https://africasacountry.com/2026/07/what-cabo-verdes-world-cup-debut-means-for-african-football","container-title":"africasacountry.com"},{"id":"source-7fd9ac70f693","type":"webpage","title":"â€˜Tactically completeâ€™ Spain vs an inspired Messi-led Argentina. Who will win the World Cup final?","URL":"https://theconversation.com/tactically-complete-spain-vs-an-inspired-messi-led-argentina-who-will-win-the-world-cup-final-287372","container-title":"theconversation.com"},{"id":"source-ace8279aafff","type":"webpage","title":"World Cup VAR controversies show why human referees should decide where potential fouls begin","URL":"https://theconversation.com/world-cup-var-controversies-show-why-human-referees-should-decide-where-potential-fouls-begin-287676","container-title":"theconversation.com"},{"id":"source-91fa33b0cb6b","type":"webpage","title":"Racism at the World Cup is an ugly reality. How to understand where it comes from","URL":"https://theconversation.com/racism-at-the-world-cup-is-an-ugly-reality-how-to-understand-where-it-comes-from-287297","container-title":"theconversation.com"},{"id":"source-f9da012a6679","type":"webpage","title":"Odyssey to Africaâ€™s last colony","URL":"https://africasacountry.com/2026/07/odyssey-to-africas-last-colony","container-title":"africasacountry.com"}],"context":["[1] Geometry, football and the World Cup final (theconversation.com, 2026-07-17T14:08:05Z) - <p>Spain will play Argentina in the Fifa World Cup final on Sunday. The highly anticipated match takes place in New Jerseyâ€™s MetLife Stadium, with the teams vying for the most coveted prize in football.</p> <p>Attacking flair and physicality will no doubt play a role. But we could also see an underappreciated element of football come to the fore: <a href=\"https://theconversation.com/topics/geometry-8213\">geometry</a>.</p> <p>In their <a href=\"https://www.reuters.com/sports/soccer/spain-choke-life-out-france-reach-world-cup-final-2026-07-14/\">2-0 World Cup semi-final win over France</a>, Spain formed neat passing triangles around the French players. This sometimes made it look as if Spain had extra people on the field. </p> <p>Wherever a Spanish player received the ball, two teammates appeared at different angles â€“ with their French opponents seemingly always arriving a fraction too late.</p> <p>The triangle mattered not because it was neat, but because it gave Spain choice and France uncertainty. Each pass forced a French defender to commit to one possible future: press the ball, block the inside route, or protect the space behind. By the time that choice was made, the ball had often moved and the geometry had changed again.</p> <p>Spain were not simply finding space. They were controlling when and where the next space would appear.</p> <h2>Forcing choices</h2> <p>Put three attackers in a straight line and one defender can often block both supporting players. But move one supporting player sideways and the passing lines separate.</p> <p>The defender must choose which route to close. Press the ball and a pass can go around them. Protect the inside route and the outside may open. If another defender steps across, a Spanish player can attack the space left behind.</p> <p>A useful football triangle does not need equal sides or 60-degree angles. Its best shape changes with defendersâ€™ positions, body orientation, ball speed and the space available.</p> <p>A narrow triangle is easy to squeeze because one defender may block both lanes. A wide one makes the pass travel farther, giving an opponent more time to intercept it.</p> <p>In their semi-final match against France, Spain found the middle ground again and again â€“ close enough for quick combinations, but far enough apart to stretch Franceâ€™s press.</p> <h2>A pitch made of probabilities</h2> <p>Football geometry is a problem of time as well as space. A passing lane is not open simply because no defender is standing in it. The key question is whether the ball reaches the receiver before an opponent reaches the interception point. </p> <p>A diagram shows where players are â€“ but a useful model of the game asks where players can get to. <a href=\"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-021-97537-4\">Research using player-tracking data</a> estimates control of the pitch from playersâ€™ position, direction, speed and acceleration. The result is a constantly changing map of who is most likely to reach each area first. </p> <p>Pedro Porroâ€™s <a href=\"https://apnews.com/article/france-spain-world-cup-score-87fb7740fa552edf4bfd28d0e8727c23\">58th-minute semi-final goal</a> offered a compact example when, after exchanging passes with teammate Dani Olmo, he continued his run to score Spainâ€™s second. The first pass shifted the defendersâ€™ focus. Porroâ€™s movement created a new angle for the return.</p> <h2>A living network</h2> <p>Three-player combinations make this idea even harder to defend. Spainâ€™s strength was not just one triangle, but the speed with which several overlapped. A midfielder could be one corner of multiple shapes, connected to a defender, full-back, winger and another midfielder.</p> <p><a href=\"https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371%2Fjournal.pone.0171156\">Passing-network research</a> describes players as points and passes as links. Yet a final network diagram for a given football match misses something important: Spain were rebuilding those links with every movement and first touch they made. </p> <p>The value of a pass is not only where the ball goes, but how it changes what could happen next. That is why ten sideways passes are not automatically better than one pass that removes two defenders.</p> <h2>The shape that conquered basketball</h2> <p>Triangles are not unique to football. Under head coach Phil Jackson (1987-1998), US basketball team the Chicago Bulls made frequent use of a tactic called <a href=\"https://www.hoophall.com/hall-of-famers/tex-winter\">Tex Winterâ€™s triangle offence</a>. This places three players on one side of the court to create passing, cutting and scoring routes. </p> <p>The sport was different but the principle was the same: triangles preserve alternatives. Close one route and another remains; follow the ball and space opens elsewhere.</p> <p>In their match against Spain, Franceâ€™s problem was not simply a lack of running. Pressing works only when several defenders move together and close connected passing options. </p> <p>After the match, Kylian MbappÃ© said there had been <a href=\"https://www.reuters.com/sports/soccer/spain-choke-life-out-france-reach-world-cup-final-2026-07-14/\">â€œa lack of communicationâ€ in Franceâ€™s pressing</a>. Spain kept moving before France could reorganise.</p> <p>A triangle on a tactics board does nothing by itself. Its power depends on players scanning before receiving, opening their bodies, and using the first touch to preserve the option of more than one next action. </p> <p>The deeper contest was between Spainâ€™s speed of reconfiguration and Franceâ€™s speed of recognition. Spain kept changing the problem faster than France could solve it. The triangles were visible â€“ but their real advantage was control over what could happen next.</p> <p>Will we see a similar approach deployed in the final? Argentina will no doubt be closely studying their opponentâ€™s tactics from other matches. But geometry will still play a prominent role â€“ and may even prove a deciding factor.</p><img src=\"https://counter.theconversation.com/content/287792/count.gif\" alt=\"The Conversation\" width=\"1\" height=\"1\" /> <p class=\"fine-print\"><em><span>Conor Boland does not work for, consult, own shares in or receive funding from any company or organisation that would benefit from this article, and has disclosed no relevant affiliations beyond their academic appointment.</span></em></p>","[2] What Cabo Verdeâ€™s World Cup debut means for African football (africasacountry.com, 2026-07-10T06:15:41.504236Z) - It happened. Cabo Verde lost to Argentina and exited the 2026 FIFA World Cup. And I couldnâ€™t be prouder. As a former football player, lifelong lover of the beautiful game, and Cabo Verdean-American, watching Cabo Verde compete on the world stage for the first time felt bigger than sports. What this team represented went far beyond the final score. At the start of the tournament, predictive models gave Cabo Verde almost no chance against former world champions Spain. The result: a stunning 0â€“0 draw anchored by seven saves from goalkeeper Josimar â€œVozinhaâ€ Dias. Against Uruguay, analysts dismissed Cabo Verdeâ€™s disciplined defensive strategy as no match for Marcelo Bielsaâ€™s aggressive attacking system. Commentators insisted Cabo Verde would never score. The result: a thrilling 2â€“2 draw, including the nationâ€™s first-ever World Cup goal from midfielder Kevin Pina. Then came another scoreless draw against Saudi Arabia, securing Cabo Verde a second-place group finish and a historic place as the smallest nation ever to reach the knockout rounds. By the time Cabo Verde faced defending champions Argentina, the world was paying attention and rooting for the underdogs. And for a moment, many of us believed the impossible could happen. When Deroy Duarte equalized to make it 1â€“1, we felt hope. When Sidny Lopes Cabral scored again to make it 2â€“2, belief swept across the world. Even after Argentina secured a 3â€“2 victory in extra time, Cabo Verde exited the tournament undefeated at the 90-minute mark against three former world champions. The loss hurt deeply. But not our pride. We may not have won on the scoreboard, but we won the respect of football fans around the world. In the days following the match, widely shared reports estimated that the Argentina versus Cabo Verde match attracted as many as 2.7 billion viewers worldwide. While those figures have not yet been officially verified by FIFA, the extraordinary claim reflected something that was unmistakably true: millions of people had fallen in love with Cabo Verdeâ€™s Cinderella story. For a nation of just over 500,000 people, that kind of global attention is extraordinary. Cabo Verde, an archipelago of 10 islands, was previously colonized by Portugal in the fifteenth century. It was a central port in the transatlantic slave trade before gaining independence in 1975. That independence came through the liberation struggle led by AmÃ­lcar Cabral, whose African Party for the Independence of Guinea and Cape Verde (PAIGC) fought Portuguese colonialism across both territories. Although Cabral was assassinated in 1973, two years before the independence, he helped make Cabo Verdeâ€™s independence possible, and remains one of Africaâ€™s most influential revolutionary thinkers. Today, the Cabo Verdean diaspora is estimated at roughly one million people, nearly double the resident population. More Cabo Verdeans now live in the United States, Portugal, the Netherlands, France, Brazil, Senegal, Angola, and elsewhere other than on the islands themselves. For decades, Cabo Verde existed on the margins of global football. The national team first entered FIFA World Cup qualification during the 2002 cycle and spent years competing against larger, wealthier, and better-funded football nations before finally qualifying for the 2026 tournament. The turning point came in the 2010s. The Blue Sharks qualified for their first Africa Cup of Nations in 2013 and stunned the continent by reaching the quarterfinals. Since then Cabo Verde has become regular AFCON competitors, and made their return to the quarterfinals in 2023. This was not a team built on global superstars or massive sponsorships. Many players came from lower-division clubs across Europe and the diaspora. Some were recruited through personal networks and online outreach, including LinkedIn, where the federation famously connected with Irish-based defender Roberto â€œPicoâ€ Lopes after he initially overlooked an earlier message written in Portuguese. What united the squad was discipline, camaraderie, and belief. Under manager Pedro â€œBubistaâ€ LeitÃ£o Brito, Cabo Verde played with tactical structure, composure, courage, and intelligence. In doing so, they challenged many of the assumptions often placed on African teams and Black athletes across sports: that they lack tactical discipline; that they rely more on physicality than strategy; or that they cannot compete at the highest level. Instead, Cabo Verde demonstrated that talent exists everywhere, even when visibility, infrastructure, and resources do not. One of the clearest examples of that visibility came through Vozinha. Before the World Cup, the 40-year-old goalkeeper was approaching retirement with a modest social media following of roughly 50,000 people. By the end of Cabo Verdeâ€™s historic run, he had become one of the breakout stars of the tournament and the most-followed goalkeeper in the world, with more than 26 million Instagram followers. Visibility changes careers. It creates sponsorship opportunities, attracts media attention, opens commercial doors, and reshapes how athletes, and the nations they represent, are perceived around the world. But this story is bigger than one player or one nation. The expanded World Cup also brought unprecedented visibility to African and Caribbean teams including Morocco, Senegal, Ghana, Egypt, Algeria, Tunisia, South Africa, DR Congo, CÃ´te dâ€™Ivoire, Haiti, and CuraÃ§ao. These nations were expected merely to make an appearance. Instead, they arrived prepared to compete. And that visibility matters. For a few extraordinary weeks, the world saw Cabo Verde not as a footnote, but as a nation worthy of respect, admiration, and serious attention. Together with the other African and Caribbean nations, they helped reshape the global conversation about the expanded 48-team tournament, the continued socio-political and economic realities facing emerging football nations, and the transformative power of access, opportunity, and visibility. In the words of Cabo Verde manager Bubista: â€œWe wanted to show that you can achieve great things regardless of your challenges, just as long as you have a dream and chase after it.â€ Thank you, Cabo Verde. And thank you to every African and Caribbean nation that stepped onto the world stage. Your fearlessness, resilience, and courage reminded the world that greatness has never been limited by geography. The future of African football is bright.","[3] â€˜Tactically completeâ€™ Spain vs an inspired Messi-led Argentina. Who will win the World Cup final? (theconversation.com, 2026-07-17T02:59:08Z) - <p>A record 102 matches have been played, leaving just two games to decide the <a href=\"https://theconversation.com/topics/fifa-world-cup-2026-186405\">2026 FIFA World Cup</a>: the third-place playoff (between France and England) and the final. </p> <p>The championship match, <a href=\"https://www.sbs.com.au/ondemand/collection/fifa-world-cup-2026-live-and-upcoming\">to be played on Sunday evening US time</a>, will see Spain take on Argentina in a fitting finale between <a href=\"https://inside.fifa.com/fifa-world-ranking/men\">the worldâ€™s two highest-ranked teams</a>. </p> <p>So, how did each team reach the final â€“ and how may it play out?</p> <p></p> <h2>How we got here</h2> <p>Both sides enter the final unbeaten after seven matches. </p> <p>Spain arrive as the reigning European champions and the worldâ€™s number two-ranked nation, while Argentina is the reigning Copa AmÃ©rica champion, defending World Cup holders and ranked first in the world.</p> <p>Spain are arguably <a href=\"https://www.nytimes.com/athletic/7446280/2026/07/15/spain-france-semifinal-tactical-analysis/\">the tournamentâ€™s most tactically complete side</a>. </p> <p>Luis de la Fuenteâ€™s team has been built on patient possession, relentless pressing and exceptional defensive organisation. </p> <p>They <a href=\"https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/articles/cpv3v0gkw9vo\">dismantled France 2-0 in the semi-final</a>, conceding very few clear chances, and have allowed just one goal throughout the tournament. In sports science terms, Spain have built towards peak performance for the final, after opening the tournament with a <a href=\"https://edition.cnn.com/2026/06/21/sport/spain-world-cup-ambitions-cape-verde\">draw against Cape Verde</a>. </p> <p>What has characterised the Spaniards is that no single player has stood above the rest â€“ the teamâ€™s success has been shared across the squad. </p> <p>This is a complete team coached by a manager at the peak of his powers. Six players have scored at least one goal, although Mikel Oyarzabal has scored five.</p> <p>Goalkeeper Unai SimÃ³n has been a rock and made some brilliant saves, and used intelligent positioning outside the box. Dani Olmo, despite being substituted twice, has been a constant attacking threat, while captain Rodri has also gone about his work with little fanfare, marshalling the team superbly from midfield. </p> <p></p> <h2>The Messi factor</h2> <p>Argentina is also a great team, but a different type of great team, and there is one standout player.</p> <p>There is little left to write about Lionel Messi that has not already been said. Yet the brilliance of this Argentine team is not simply built around his genius â€“ it is built around the extraordinary commitment of the ten players around him. </p> <hr> <p> <em> <strong> Read more: <a href=\"https://theconversation.com/messi-is-old-short-and-slow-how-is-he-still-dominating-at-the-world-cup-286238\">Messi is old, short and slow. How is he still dominating at the World Cup?</a> </strong> </em> </p> <hr> <p>They run for him, defend for him, sacrifice for him, and believe in him completely. </p> <p>Messi is afforded the freedom to conserve his energy, drifting into the spaces where he can do the greatest damage. His teammates carry the physical burden, allowing their captain to deliver moments of magic when they matter most. </p> <p>He is not merely Argentinaâ€™s leader â€“ he is their inspiration, their symbol, and the figure who carries the hopes of an entire nation. Throughout the tournament, whenever Argentina faced a moment of crisis, the players turned to Messi with the belief he would find a way.</p> <p>He nearly always does, and <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/football/2026/jul/16/england-appeared-to-have-lionel-messi-under-control-until-the-crucial-moments\">he did it again</a> in their unforgettable victory over England. </p> <p>That win represented more than qualification for another final. For many Argentinians, it carried the emotional weight of <a href=\"https://www.goal.com/en-au/lists/lionel-messi-argentina-england-diego-maradona-tribute-world-cup-semi-final/blt8cb51a8577016719\">Diego Maradonaâ€™s legacy</a> and offered the opportunity to defeat England on soccerâ€™s biggest stage through skill and determination rather than controversy. </p> <p>It was another defining chapter in Argentinaâ€™s remarkable World Cup story.</p> <p></p> <h2>How will the final play out?</h2> <p>Tactically, the match promises a fascinating contrast.</p> <p>Spain will seek to monopolise possession, patiently moving the ball until openings appear, while Argentina is comfortable defending compactly before striking quickly (usually through Messi).</p> <p>Whichever side controls the tempo in midfield is likely to have one hand on the trophy.</p> <p>Spain will try and play with the finesse they showed against the French, and Rodri will try to ensure they avoid a â€œstreet fightâ€, which the Argentinians may try and bait them into.</p> <p>History also adds another layer. Spain is chasing its second World Cup after the triumph in 2010, while Argentina is pursuing a fourth title and back-to-back World Cups â€“ an achievement not secured in more than six decades. </p> <p>The final therefore offers Spain the chance to cement a new era of dominance, while Argentina seek to extend one of the greatest periods in its history.</p> <p>It is a fitting climax to the tournament: the worldâ€™s number one-ranked team against the ranked team second. The defending champions against the European champions, and almost certainly Messiâ€™s final World Cup appearance.</p> <p></p> <p>In a sport now dominated by players who run all day and are increasingly similar, with an abundance of machine-like footballers and fewer players blessed with extraordinary skill and tactical understanding, this World Cup has produced some remarkable goals and performances.</p> <p>Harry Kane, Jude Bellingham, Erling Haaland and Kylian MbappÃ© have all produced moments of brilliance.</p> <p>But the final 25 minutes of the semi-final between England and Argentina, after England had taken a 1-0 lead, demonstrated that there has never been, and perhaps never will be, another player quite like Messi.</p> <p>We may be about to marvel at Messi for the last time. It is hard to believe he will not find a way to overcome this outstanding Spanish team.</p><img src=\"https://counter.theconversation.com/content/287372/count.gif\" alt=\"The Conversation\" width=\"1\" height=\"1\" /> <p class=\"fine-print\"><em><span>Steve Georgakis does not work for, consult, own shares in or receive funding from any company or organisation that would benefit from this article, and has disclosed no relevant affiliations beyond their academic appointment.</span></em></p>","[4] World Cup VAR controversies show why human referees should decide where potential fouls begin (theconversation.com, 2026-07-16T21:22:02Z) - <p>Two contested decisions at the 2026 World Cup <a href=\"https://www.reuters.com/sports/soccer/vargentina-reach-semi-finals-under-cloud-complaints-2026-07-13/\">have reignited debate</a> over how video assistant referee (VAR) reviews should work, and how far back a referee should look when deciding what counts as part of the same passage of play.</p> <p>VAR allows referees to review contentious incidents using replay footage, <a href=\"https://www.theifab.com/laws/latest/video-assistant-referee-var-protocol/\">with the human referee making the final decision</a>. Itâ€™s meant to catch â€œclear and obvious errorsâ€ or incidents that the on-field officials missed. A referee reviewing an incident must decide how far back in time the reviewable passage of play extends.</p> <p>A 2021 <a href=\"https://fis.dshs-koeln.de/en/publications/video-assistant-referees-var-the-impact-of-technology-on-decision/\">study of 2,195 competitive football matches across 13 countries</a> found referees got calls right 92.1 per cent of the time, rising to 98.3 per cent after a VAR intervention. </p> <p>But replay only supplies the evidence. It doesnâ€™t supply interpretation, responsibility or a decision about how much of the play needs to be examined to establish appropriate context. That judgment falls to the referee. VARâ€™s effectiveness depends on referees interpreting the evidence within the incidentâ€™s interactions and its relevant context.</p> <p>As researchers <a href=\"https://digital.la84.org/digital/collection/p17103coll10/id/24101/\">who study the ethical implications of AI use in soccer</a>, weâ€™ve found that two decisions from the 2026 tournament illustrate how difficult these boundary calls can be in practice.</p> <h2>Argentinaâ€™s overturned goal</h2> <p>In the round of 16 match between Argentina and Egypt, VAR overturned an Egyptian goal after reviewing an incident that occurred nearly a minute earlier.</p> <p>Egypt was leading 1-0 when Mostafa Ziko scored in the 58th minute to make it 2-0. VAR recommended an on-field review of Egyptâ€™s Marwan Attia stepping on Argentinaâ€™s Lisandro MartÃ­nezâ€™s foot <a href=\"https://observer.co.uk/news/national/article/red-cards-var-disallowed-goals-conspiracy-theories-thrive-at-the-world-cup\">17 seconds before the goal</a> and more than 80 metres from where it was scored. </p> <p>The referee reviewed the incident and cancelled the goal. Argentina came back to win the game 3-2. </p> <p>The integral question is whether that foot-stepping incident still counted as being connected to the goal a minute later, or whether play had moved on enough to be treated as over and done with.</p> <p><a href=\"https://inside.fifa.com/refereeing/news/pierluigi-collina-we-are-not-influenced\">FIFA, soccerâ€™s governing body, says</a> an attacking phase in a game has no pre-set time or distance limit. </p> <p>Providing commentary on the situation, former referee <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/football/2026/jul/08/refereeing-world-cup-fine-input-tuchel-trump-comment\">Chris Foy argued the play should count as one continuous move</a> because the ball kept travelling forward. Another former referee, <a href=\"https://www.foxsports.com/stories/soccer/egypts-goal-vs-argentina-disallowed-after-var-check-not-why-var-brought-game\">Mark Clattenburg, disagreed</a>, arguing the distance covered and the time that had passed were enough to break the connection between the foul and the goal that followed.</p> <p>Egypt also appealed for a penalty late in the match, <a href=\"https://www.espn.com/soccer/story/_/id/49312344/pierluigi-collina-defends-egypt-argentina-referee-calls-send-home-world-cup\">after Mohamed Salah went down following contact with JuliÃ¡n Ãlvarez</a>, but it didnâ€™t prompt an on-field review; <a href=\"https://inside.fifa.com/refereeing/news/pierluigi-collina-we-are-not-influenced\">FIFA said</a> the defender played the ball first before normal contact followed. </p> <p>Both decisions may be legally defensible, but their different treatment shows why officials should explain where their review began, why the earlier act remained relevant and why one incident reached the VAR monitor while the other did not.</p> <h2>Haaland push overturns Norwayâ€™s goal</h2> <p>A second case, from Norwayâ€™s World Cup quarterfinal against England, raises a similar question: does a VAR review apply before the ball is back in play? </p> <p>TorbjÃ¸rn Heggem appeared to put Norway ahead in the 55th minute of the game, scoring on a rebound from a corner kick. </p> <p>But replays showed that as the corner was being delivered, Norwayâ€™s <a href=\"https://www.the-independent.com/sport/football/norway-england-goal-erling-haaland-foul-var-world-cup-b3013299.html\">Erling Haaland had extended both his arms and pushed Englandâ€™s Elliot Anderson to the ground</a>. The two had been <a href=\"https://www.si.com/soccer/how-new-world-cup-rule-england-vs-norway-world-cup-quarterfinal\">jostling for position</a> prior to the moment of contact, and Haaland appeared to be reacting to Anderson.</p> <p>The ball was not yet in play when the push occurred, but officials judged it had a direct causal link to the goal that followed: Haaland remained involved as the ball arrived from the corner kick, which fell to Patrick Berg. His shot was saved by goalkeeper Jordan Pickford, and the rebound fell to Heggem, who scored.</p> <p>Under a World Cup clarification, an attacking offence before a restart of play <a href=\"https://www.theifab.com/news/the-ifab-clarifies-var-protocol-for-attacking-team-offences-before-the-ball-is-in/\">may be reviewed if it directly affects a goal</a>. After review, the goal was cancelled and the corner retaken. Norway lost the match 2-1 after extra time.</p> <p>Questions were nonetheless asked about when the review began, since it did not appear to include Andersonâ€™s own physical duel with Haaland in the moments before the push. That earlier jostling does not necessarily mean Anderson provoked Haaland, but it is a reminder that the referee wasnâ€™t reviewing an isolated fact off a VAR screen. </p> <p>Neither case necessarily proves the refereesâ€™ decisions wrong, but both show that defining the relevant incident remains part of human refereeing.</p> <h2>Where does an incident begin?</h2> <p>VARâ€™s core problem is, in fact, defining the event under review: how far it extends through time and across the pitch, and where the causal connection ends. This requires reading whether ball possession, player positions and opportunities to challenge still carry the effects of an earlier incident, or whether the game has moved on and reset. </p> <p>Too narrow a frame isolates an action from the broader context that makes it significant. Too broad a frame drags in remote events after a new phase of the game has begun. </p> <p>Footage is not self-explanatory. Referees and video officials choose duration, angle, speed and relevant interactions, turning play into a visual narrative of the game. This process can test the refereeâ€™s impression, but it can also shape what appears on screen as â€œthe incident.â€</p> <p>A <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1186/s41235-018-0105-8\">study of 88 elite referees from five countries</a> found slow motion produced more severe sanctions than normal speed. An accurate slow-motion fragment can produce a contextually incomplete judgment when compared with the whole event.</p> <p>Consider two children, A and B, competing for a reward. A provokes B in ways a parent, C, doesnâ€™t see. When B pushes A, C sees only the reaction. B is still responsible, but the fuller context can change how the act is understood.</p> <p>This is the test we suggest: could an omitted interaction change perceptions about what offence occurred, who was responsible or what consequences should follow? By that measure, reviews should end once possession, position and opportunities to challenge have been genuinely reset.</p> <h2>Making reviews fairer</h2> <p>We propose three changes to make VAR reviews clearer and fairer. </p> <p>First, the refereeâ€™s initial call on the field, including any decision to let play continue, should establish what actually needs reviewing. Second, the replay should be checked against that specific call. Third, the human referee should interpret what the replay shows using the relevant passage of play, <a href=\"https://www.theifab.com/laws-of-the-game-documents/?language=all&amp;year=2026%2F27\">FIFAâ€™s official rules</a> and footballâ€™s shared norms for what counts as fair.</p> <p>Footballâ€™s law-making body <a href=\"https://www.theifab.com/laws/latest/video-assistant-referee-var-protocol/\">allows competitions to publish public VAR explanations</a>. To preserve match flow, FIFA should add a public post-match note for consequential reviews and checks, laying out the timing and distance of the incident, and the intervention threshold, including whether play continued or reset.</p> <p>Referees should also judge intensity at normal speed, and only use slow motion to confirm that contact happened, not to judge how hard it was. The technology should support human judgment, not replace it. The clarity of an isolated replay must never be mistaken for the completeness of a fair judgment, since only a human referee can supply that judgment.</p><img src=\"https://counter.theconversation.com/content/287676/count.gif\" alt=\"The Conversation\" width=\"1\" height=\"1\" /> <p class=\"fine-print\"><em><span>The authors do not work for, consult, own shares in or receive funding from any company or organisation that would benefit from this article, and have disclosed no relevant affiliations beyond their academic appointment.</span></em></p>","[5] Racism at the World Cup is an ugly reality. How to understand where it comes from (theconversation.com, 2026-07-16T14:28:15Z) - <figure><img src=\"https://images.theconversation.com/files/748358/original/file-20260716-57-4yz928.jpg?ixlib=rb-4.1.0&amp;rect=0%2C1%2C6452%2C4301&amp;q=45&amp;auto=format&amp;w=1050&amp;h=700&amp;fit=crop\" /><figcaption><span class=\"caption\">Racism was in the news once again at the World Cup. But it comes in many forms.</span> <span class=\"attribution\"><a class=\"source\" href=\"https://www.pexels.com/photo/a-man-sitting-on-a-football-field-5247136/\">Thirdman/Pexels</a></span></figcaption></figure><p>No African teams made it past the quarter-finals of the 2026 menâ€™s football <a href=\"https://www.fifa.com/en/tournaments/mens/worldcup/canadamexicousa2026/standings\">World Cup</a>, but their strategic and tactical play left a <a href=\"https://novanews.co.za/african-teams-silence-critics-with-strong-world-cup-showing/#google_vignette\">lasting</a> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/shorts/eAF-HVdyWs0\">impression</a>. </p> <p>The success of African teams doesnâ€™t come out of the blue, itâ€™s the result of a long <a href=\"https://theconversation.com/world-cup-history-a-timeline-of-africas-greatest-moments-on-the-field-286908\">process</a>. Itâ€™s therefore surprising that African football remains underrated and downgraded by experts, often in a manner that reveals their ignorance both to African football and history. At times, their comments have a highly problematic racist tone.</p> <p>This was the case with former star player <a href=\"https://www.transfermarkt.co.za/bastian-schweinsteiger/profil/spieler/2514\">Bastian Schweinsteiger</a>, now a longtime commentator for a German TV network. Ahead of a group stage match against CÃ´te dâ€™Ivoire, he was asked what Germanyâ€™s team could expect. He <a href=\"https://www.espn.co.uk/football/story/_/id/49173319/jurgen-klopp-refuses-question-bastian-schweinsteiger-remarks-ivory-coast-play-style\">responded</a>:</p> <blockquote> <p>A bit African football, a bit unorthodox, a bit wild â€¦ perhaps also not so conditioned by tactics. We must be prepared for it to be unpredictable.</p> </blockquote> <p>Setting aside the fact that the German team was often the weaker side â€“ both tactically and in terms of style of play â€“ this characterisation is inappropriate and offensive. Itâ€™s based on a <a href=\"https://theconversation.com/racial-stereotypes-of-african-footballers-persist-a-world-cup-is-a-good-time-to-talk-about-them-286673\">stereotyped</a>, colonialist view of Africa.</p> <p></p> <p>On the one hand, Schweinsteiger <a href=\"https://www.dw.com/en/schweinsteigers-ivory-coast-comments-spark-racism-debate/a-77674794\">faced criticism that his statement was racist</a>. </p> <p>As Cote dâ€™Ivoireâ€™s manager, Emerse Fae, <a href=\"https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/articles/cx2x0ev2deqo\">said</a>:</p> <blockquote> <p>It is odd he would speak that way. We could call it racist â€¦ I can only hope it is a clumsy statement, rather than something going on in his mind.</p> </blockquote> <figure> <iframe width=\"440\" height=\"260\" src=\"https://www.youtube.com/embed/Kzgtcg8lREA?wmode=transparent&amp;start=0\" frameborder=\"0\" allowfullscreen=\"\"></iframe> </figure> <p>On the other hand, there were many people on online forums and <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DaLU8CfoRO8/\">social media</a> who wondered why Schweinsteigerâ€™s statement was considered racist, <a href=\"https://www.reuters.com/sports/soccer/schweinsteiger-rejects-racism-claims-broadcaster-ard-backs-pundit-2026-06-26/\">as did</a> his broadcaster, ARD. </p> <p>Schweinsteiger <a href=\"https://www.reuters.com/sports/soccer/schweinsteiger-rejects-racism-claims-broadcaster-ard-backs-pundit-2026-06-26/\">responded weakly</a> to the criticism:</p> <blockquote> <p>I was talking about football, not people. This is a football analysis â€“ nothing more, nothing less. </p> </blockquote> <p>I often hear this kind of downplaying when football in Africa and its people are subtly devalued. As an <a href=\"https://www.sobi.uni-passau.de/en/southeast-asian-studies/team/postdocs/dr-christian-ungruhe\">anthropologist</a>, my <a href=\"https://www.manchesterhive.com/display/9781526120274/9781526120274.xml?rskey=1NdWtS&amp;result=1&amp;print\">research</a> focuses on the migration of football players from various parts of Africa and on the forces that have shaped the evolution of African football, especially the modern game. A key <a href=\"https://brill.com/view/journals/afdi/6/2/article-p196_4.xml\">focus</a> is on racialisation, othering, and racism experienced by players. </p> <p>The <a href=\"https://www.manchesterhive.com/display/9781526120274/9781526120274.00015.xml\">research shows</a> that African footballers, and Black players more generally, face different forms of racism. </p> <p>On social media, they frequently encounter open and hostile rejection and insults. A recent example is the offensive post by Paraguayan senator Celeste Amarilla, after her countryâ€™s loss to France. She <a href=\"https://www.nytimes.com/athletic/7427643/2026/07/07/kylian-mbappe-france-paraguay-racism-senator-amarilla/\">described Kylian MbappÃ©</a> as a</p> <blockquote> <p>colonised Cameroonian, desperately trying to pass himself off as French â€¦ resentful, arrogant and ugly. </p> </blockquote> <p>Players from Africa also face <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.33196/juridikum202502020701\">structural disadvantages</a> in the football industry. This is evident in <a href=\"https://libra.unine.ch/server/api/core/bitstreams/28690f7b-85a1-46bb-89c0-3173d058fedc/content\">shorter contracts and lower salaries</a>, as well as <a href=\"https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/10.1177/10126902221081125\">peripheral playing positions</a> and less consideration for <a href=\"https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/full/10.1177/1012690216656807\">coaching and administrative roles</a>, as various studies show. </p> <p>After all, they are also constantly confronted with more subtle forms of everyday racism, <a href=\"https://www.nzz.ch/sport/mist-nun-geht-die-debatte-wieder-los-was-darf-man-denn-noch-sagen-dachte-der-afrika-experte-nach-schweinsteigers-aussagen-zum-wilden-fussball-ld.10013653\">which manifests</a> itself in their abilities and qualities being devalued. They are often reduced to their physical appearance, their intelligence denied.</p> <figure> <iframe width=\"440\" height=\"260\" src=\"https://www.youtube.com/embed/Hlz3HY2uZv4?wmode=transparent&amp;start=0\" frameborder=\"0\" allowfullscreen=\"\"></iframe> </figure> <p>Everyday racism is often expressed subtly and white people <a href=\"https://www.tagesspiegel.de/sport/rassistische-zuschreibungen-aus-der-kolonialzeit-wie-der-fussball-afrika-klischees-am-leben-halt-15761487.html\">like Schweinsteiger</a> are often not aware of its racist connotation. Therefore, <a href=\"https://www.spiegel.de/sport/vorurteile-im-fussball-die-wilden-waren-wir-deutschen-a-3824b4af-b986-48d8-85bc-88ddac218ce1\">the question</a> of whether Bastian Schweinsteiger is a racist or not is not the crucial one (he most likely is not).</p> <p>The key question is rather why these stereotypes are repeatedly expressed in football and why, for many people, they seem to reflect reality rather than appear problematic. To understand this, we need to do justice to Africaâ€™s diversity and further look at the continentâ€™s colonial history.</p> <h2>Colonial stereotypes</h2> <p>For one thing, simplistic statements like Schweinsteigerâ€™s disparage football across an entire continent without making any distinctions. There is just as little such thing as an <a href=\"https://www.moroccoworldnews.com/2026/06/325902/schweinsteigers-wild-african-football-remark-repeats-a-tired-stereotype/\">â€œAfricanâ€ style of football</a> as there is a â€œEuropeanâ€ one. This does not do justice to a complex reality and it reveals either ignorance or indifference. </p> <p>Second, describing African football as â€œunorthodox,â€ â€œwild,â€ and â€œnot characterised by tacticsâ€, <a href=\"https://www.reuters.com/sports/soccer/racist-commentary-about-black-players-comes-under-scrutiny-world-cup-2026-07-06/\">perpetuates stereotypes</a> from the colonial era that extend beyond the sport. My <a href=\"https://pure.eur.nl/ws/portalfiles/portal/47331921/ungruhe_fav_126788.pdf\">study</a> of African footballers in Germany shows how the players are vulnerable to broader, structural vulnerabilities in Europe.</p> <p>How many view African migrant footballers is grounded in colonial thinking. European colonisation legitimised itself by <a href=\"https://www.dandc.eu/en/article/oppressive-imperial-powers-claimed-be-spreading-civilisation-whilst-promoting-racism\">ranking people</a>. The enlightened European, capable of reflection, and the uneducated African, who must be tamed. </p> <p>Starting in the 1920s, colonial administrators deliberately <a href=\"https://phambo.wiser.org.za/files/seminars/Alegi2010.pdf\">used football to subjugate Africans</a>. Sport was used to teach team spirit and rules to the allegedly naÃ¯ve, raw and perhaps threatening African in order to turn him into a person loyal to the colony.</p> <p>Openly showing resistance was dangerous. But interestingly, this happened on the football field. Studies <a href=\"https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/africa/article/abs/kickin-it-leisure-politics-and-football-in-colonial-zanzibar-1900s1950s/A97494FF2D4FEB7BFA1252B4A11A6309\">outline</a> how some Africans didnâ€™t try to play by the rules. They tried to make feints, dribbles, and tricks â€“ on the one hand to entertain their own people, but also to display defiance. </p> <p>During the struggle for independence, football was then used by some African countries to <a href=\"https://www.jstor.org/stable/43305103?seq=1\">foster a sense</a> of national and pan-African unity. After all, colonial borders had been drawn arbitrarily. National football teams were important, but so was the style of play â€“ integrity and technical skill presented an alternative to the colonial image. </p> <p>Over the years, national playing styles have developed in various ways. They are influenced by cultural values, transnational linkages and the game becoming professionalised. However, the original mix â€“ powerful bodies, skillful dribbling, and a childlike, naÃ¯ve approach â€“ has become ingrained in European minds.</p> <h2>Slow poison</h2> <p>At first glance, subtle disparaging remarks like Schweinsteigerâ€™s may not seem to be as racist as the open insults and degrading comments seen every day in football and society. But they are hurtful. And because they are firmly embedded in the social knowledge of European countries, they are <a href=\"https://www.sueddeutsche.de/projekte/artikel/jetzt/tah-schweinsteiger-weltmeisterschaft-rassismus-e998278/?reduced=true\">hard to erase</a>. </p> <hr> <p> <em> <strong> Read more: <a href=\"https://theconversation.com/racial-stereotypes-of-african-footballers-persist-a-world-cup-is-a-good-time-to-talk-about-them-286673\">Racial stereotypes of African footballers persist. A World Cup is a good time to talk about them</a> </strong> </em> </p> <hr> <p>Rather, they are a creeping poison that, when constantly reproduced, shapes a societyâ€™s collective consciousness. As my research has <a href=\"https://brill.com/view/journals/afdi/6/2/article-p196_4.xml\">found</a>, such remarks not only disparage football in Africa but also shape the general perception of a continent that is allegedly backward at every level. </p> <p>To tackle the powerful colonial-era image of an African inferiority, it is important to take action against all forms of racist disparagement in football and beyond, whether they are unintentional, subtle, or explicit.</p><img src=\"https://counter.theconversation.com/content/287297/count.gif\" alt=\"The Conversation\" width=\"1\" height=\"1\" /> <p class=\"fine-print\"><em><span>Christian Ungruhe receives funding from the University of Passau. </span></em></p>","[6] Odyssey to Africaâ€™s last colony (africasacountry.com, 2026-07-17T15:16:23.012431Z) - In the run-up to its theatrical release, Christopher Nolanâ€™s The Odyssey became embroiled in online polemics after Elon Musk attacked the movieâ€™s supposed â€œwokeâ€ casting. Yet beyond this contrived spectacle lies a far more important criticism to be made of the filmmakers: their decision to shoot part of the movie in Africaâ€™s last colony, Western Sahara. Enjoying generous subsidies from the Moroccan state, they lent legitimacy to its illegal occupation regime. As Nolan and his crew filmed along the coast around the port city of Dakhla last summer, an open letter condemning the move was signed by prominent figures in world cinema, including Javier Bardem, Pedro AlmodÃ³var, and Paul Laverty. â€œMr. Nolan filmed there without the consent of the Sahrawi people,â€ it read, in reference to the majority nationality in Western Sahara. â€œThe only consent he received came from the occupying force: Morocco.â€ In particular, Oscar-winning actor Bardem did not hold back. As he posted the letter on his Instagram account, he added: â€œFor 50 years, Morocco has occupied Western Sahara, expelling the Sahrawi people from their cities. Dakhla is one of them, converted by the Moroccan occupiers into a tourist destination and now a film set, always with the aim of erasing the Sahrawi identity of the city.â€ Covering an area the size of Britain, Western Sahara is designated as a â€œnon-self-governing territoryâ€ by the United Nations and remains on its official list of territories still awaiting decolonization. Retained by Francisco Francoâ€™s Spain even as other European colonies won their freedom, it did not gain independence even after the dictatorâ€™s demise in 1975. Instead, neighboring Morocco and Mauritania invaded â€” with at least 40 percent of the Sahrawi population at the time fleeing to neighboring Algeria to escape the Moroccan air forceâ€™s bombing campaign. Half a century later, 173,000 Sahrawis still remain in Algerian refugee camps. The native Sahrawis living under Moroccan occupation are subject to what Freedom House categorizes as among the least free political systems on the planet. The Odyssey is the first major Hollywood production to shoot scenes in Western Sahara. This would have been unthinkable prior to 2020, when President Donald Trump broke with decades of US foreign policy and recognized Moroccoâ€™s illegally established sovereignty over the territory. The decision was part of a quid pro quo in exchange for Moroccoâ€™s normalization of ties with Israel. Now Nolan and crewâ€™s four-day shoot in Dakhla illustrates how quickly Hollywood studios have moved to exploit the opportunities created by Trumpâ€™s transactional diplomacy. This subsidized occupation cinema is yet another symptom of the breakdown of whatever remained of a rules-based liberal order. For Moroccan officials, the presence of The Odyssey crew was a propaganda coup. They have made it clear they see it as just the beginning of Dakhlaâ€™s development as a base for international film productions. This is despite the fact that the wider Western Sahara remains the site of an ongoing armed conflict between the countryâ€™s military and the Sahrawi pro-independence movement, the Polisario Front. Last month, three Polisario fighters were killed in a Moroccan drone attack close to the 2,700-kilometer sand berm that separates Moroccan-controlled territory from the desert areas held by Polisario. If the construction of the vast defensive berm in the 1980s was Moroccoâ€™s attempt to entrench its military control over Western Sahara, the transformation of Dakhla into a tourist destination and green energy hub in recent years aims to consolidate the occupation as an irreversible economic reality. From the Moroccan leadershipâ€™s perspective, Sahrawi independence will look increasingly unrealistic if it can develop the territory in conjunction with international investors. It is also incentivizing Moroccan settlers to move to the territory with generous subsidies and jobs. The clearest expression of this strategy is Moroccoâ€™s Atlantic Initiative, which looks to provide landlocked countries in the Sahel region with maritime access to the Atlantic via a new â‚¬1.3 billion port facility currently under construction in Dakhla (and set to be operational by 2028). Positioning the occupied city as a key logistics hub for northwestern Africa, the project aims to further integrate Western Sahara into regional trade networks. Tourism is also key to normalizing Moroccan control, with Dakhla in particular recast as an international destination for kitesurfing and ecotourism. On a 2022 visit, Ivanka Trump and Jared Kushner were photographed at one of the growing number of high-end hotels on Dakhla peninsula, as well as on the sweeping Atlantic coastline that would later attract the film crew shooting The Odyssey . The coupleâ€™s holiday photo shoot offered a vision of luxury resorts and leisure in which the military occupation and indigenous Sahrawi population were erased from view. Airline Ryanairâ€™s announcement in 2024 that it was opening new direct routes connecting Spain with Dakhla and the Sahrawi capital El AaiÃºn marked a further expansion of this model of occupation tourism â€” even as the European Commission informed airlines that routes involving Western Sahara would not be covered by the terms of the EU-Moroccan aviation agreement. At the same time, international journalists, trade unionists, and human rights defenders have looked to break the Moroccan media blockade by boarding these low-cost flights over the last 18 months, only to be detained at the airport or arrested when they made contact with local Sahrawi activists. Footage from last year even showed a left-wing delegation from the European Parliament being physically blocked from disembarking a Ryanair flight by Moroccan security forces. The Odyssey â€™s subsidized production forms part of this same effort to establish Dakhla as an international â€” but firmly Moroccan â€” destination while limiting scrutiny of the occupation itself. Yet as news of the Dakhla shoot emerged last summer, Sahrawi filmmakers, journalists, and activists took to social media to contrast the free rein afforded to Nolanâ€™s Hollywood production with the systematic repression they faced in trying to document the Moroccan stateâ€™s human rights violations, or simply in exercising creative freedom. â€œI grew up in the Sahrawi refugee camps in Algeria and today, as a Sahrawi filmmaker from the occupied city of Dakhla, I cannot freely enter my homeland to tell my own stories,â€ director Brahim Chagaf posted as part of the online campaign organized by The Western Sahara International Film Festival. â€œThat is the great contradiction behind this landscape: while a few privileged people like Nolan can turn it into a movie, others are still waiting for the day when we can simply return to it.â€ Campaigner Ghalia Djimiâ€™s message was even starker: â€œMr Nolan: I am a human rights defender. Morocco disappeared me for 3 years and 7 months in a secret prison in occupied El AaiÃºn.â€ Her experience is far from exceptional. In its latest 2024 report, human rights NGO CODESA catalogued dozens of abuses carried out by Moroccan security forces that year. These included the repeated violent suppression of peaceful protests, the harassment and arbitrary detention of activists, and the suspicious deaths in custody of three Sahrawi civilians. In November 2023, the UN Working Group on Arbitrary Detention concluded that the detentions of two dozen Sahrawi activists and journalists, held ever since the Gdeim Izik protest camp in 2010, were illegal. It also found that in the case of 18 student activists detained in 2016, torture was used to extract confessions. As The Odyssey opens at the global box office, one of the Gdeim Izik prisoners, EnaÃ¢ma Asfari, is currently in the fourth week of an indefinite hunger strike. In calling for his immediate release last month, Frontline Defenders said it was â€œconcerned about reports describing medical neglect, reprisals and other forms of ill-treatment against Sahrawi human rights defenders in prison.â€ â€œWhen Christopher Nolan steps on the red carpet on his way to the premiereâ€™s screening, he will also be stepping on International Law,â€ insisted MarÃ­a CarriÃ³n, executive director of The Western Sahara International Film Festival, last month. â€œWe ask the public to treat this film as they would a movie made in occupied Ukraine with [Vladimir] Putinâ€™s permits, or in the illegal settlements in Palestine with [Benjamin] Netanyahuâ€™s blessing.â€ A series of rulings from the European Unionâ€™s highest court back this claim. Over the past decade, the Court of Justice of the European Union has repeatedly found that Western Sahara is a territory with a â€œseparate and distinctâ€ status to Morocco and that legally its resources cannot be exploited without the consent of the Sahrawi people. Those judgments concern specific EU-Moroccan trade agreements, which the EU has tried to revive despite successive rulings from its own courts that they contravened international law. But the rulings raise broader questions about the responsibilities of international companies operating in the occupied territory, including film studios. Given his back catalog, Nolanâ€™s brilliance as a writer and director is unquestionable. Yet with The Odyssey , he and Universal Pictures have set a dangerous precedent as they pioneered a new form of filmmaking for our Trumpian age: occupation cinema. A country subject to a brutal colonization and a system of effective apartheid is not a legitimate backdrop for either international tourism or a Hollywood blockbuster."],"accountableAttention":{"rule":"make-the-click-preserve-the-warrant","headlineMustPreserve":["attribution","modality","scope","uncertainty","correction state"],"forbiddenStrengthenings":["source claim to newsroom fact","may/could/alleged to does/did/proves","single case to trend","correlation to causation","reader demand to evidence"],"correctionSurfacePolicy":"Any later correction to a cited claim must propagate to body copy, headline, social cards, feeds, JSON-LD, and syndicated packets."},"constraints":["This packet is research intake, not a publishable article.","Only current-provider claims are admitted as source-bound factual evidence.","Prior-corpus excerpts are contextual leads until separately sourced and admitted.","Publication still requires a licensed editorial state, exact-manuscript judgment, and explicit publishing action."]}

CURRENT BODY:
Spain and Argentina will meet at MetLife Stadium for the 2026 FIFA World Cup final, a match [Geometry, football and the World Cup final](https://theconversation.com/geometry-football-and-the-world-cup-final-287792) identifies as a clash between the worldâ€™s two highest-ranked nations. Both teams arrive undefeated after seven matches, though they approach the contest with fundamentally different tactical philosophies, according to [â€˜Tactically completeâ€™ Spain vs an inspired Messi-led Argentina. Who will win the World Cup final?](https://theconversation.com/tactically-complete-spain-vs-an-inspired-messi-led-argentina-who-will-win-the-world-cup-final-287372)

In their 2-0 semi-final win against France, Spain employed fluid passing triangles to manipulate the pitch. These shapes were not defined by uniform angles but by their ability to force defensive choices. Every pass compelled French defenders to commit to pressing the ball, blocking interior lanes, or protecting space behind them. Because Spanish players reconfigured their positions rapidly, France could not adapt its defensive structure quickly enough. Following the match, Kylian MbappÃ© cited a "lack of communication" as the primary reason for his team's inability to organize against these sequences.

## The geometry of football triangles

Effective spatial play relies on the ability to preserve multiple alternatives. While standard passing-network research often maps players as fixed points, the movement of a squad like Spainâ€™s reveals that these networks are dynamic, rebuilt with every first touch and adjustment. A useful triangle does not require equal sides or 60-degree angles; its ideal geometry is fluid, shifting in response to an opponent's body orientation, the speed of the ball, and the available space.

By remaining close enough to facilitate rapid combinations while staying far enough apart to stretch a press, Spain creates a living network that forces uncertainty onto the opposition. The team treats the pitch as a map of probabilities rather than fixed zones, using the threat of potential player locations to control where space appears. Pedro Porroâ€™s 58th-minute goal against France demonstrated this; his combination with Dani Olmo shifted the defendersâ€™ focus and opened the lane for his own scoring run. The underlying strategy mirrors the principles used by the Chicago Bulls under Phil Jackson, where the triangle offense was designed to keep defensive options limited by ensuring at least one route remained open.

## The geometry of the final showdown

The championship match features a structural contrast between Spainâ€™s collective system and Argentinaâ€™s reliance on Lionel Messi. Spain is noted for patient possession and rigorous defensive organization, having conceded only one goal throughout the tournament. Six different players have scored for the Spanish squad, reflecting a shared team responsibility and a character built across the tournament. In contrast, Argentina is constructed around a framework of physical sacrifice; ten players manage the defensive burden to allow Messi the freedom to conserve energy for decisive moments. This model has repeatedly served Argentina in high-pressure matches, including their semi-final against England, where Messi was afforded the freedom to drift into spaces where he could do the greatest damage.

The final will test whether Spainâ€™s speed of reconfiguration can overcome Argentinaâ€™s speed of recognition. Spainâ€™s captain, Rodri, will likely attempt to maintain the team's technical tempo to avoid the physical "street fight" that Argentina may seek to induce. Spain aims to cement a new era of dominance following their 2010 title, while Argentina pursues a fourth title and a back-to-back championship sequence not seen in six decades. 

As these approaches collide, the match hinges on whether Spain can continue to force defensive dilemmas through geometry, or if Argentinaâ€™s tactical flexibility and reliance on Messiâ€™s energy-efficient impact will allow them to dictate the gameâ€™s terms. The deeper contest, therefore, is not merely a clash of squads but a battle over the control of time and space. Spainâ€™s reliance on the passing triangle forces a problem that the opposition must solve in real-time, while Argentinaâ€™s ability to defend compactly allows them to wait for the specific, high-leverage moments where Messi can bypass structural defenses entirely. Whether Spainâ€™s triangles remain a sustainable mechanism for pressure or whether Argentinaâ€™s patient defensive shell proves impenetrable will determine the final outcome of the 2026 World Cup.
